#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ollama-dispatch-preflight -- one GO / NO-GO gate for an ollama dispatch.

WHY THIS EXISTS
---------------
Steps 1-4 of the ollama-dispatch skill (worktree -> spec-complete TASK.md ->
both-ways-proven verify.sh -> meta-checks) were run BY HAND before every
dispatch. That is the bulk of the per-dispatch effort, and a hand-run checklist
has exactly one failure mode: a step gets skipped, and the resulting failure is
then misread for rounds as *model incapacity*. Every failure this project has
attributed to the model turned out to be spec/harness/human.

So the checklist becomes a program. Given a worktree containing TASK.md +
verify.sh (+ a reference implementation), this runs the whole scaffold and
prints ONE verdict with the exact failing reason.

DIVISION OF LABOUR -- this tool does NOT judge verify QUALITY
------------------------------------------------------------
`~/bin/verify-quality.py` already decides whether a verify is sound (fails at
baseline; asserts whole spec literals rather than a negation-matching wildcard).
This tool SHELLS OUT to it rather than carrying a second copy of that logic --
the same rule verify-quality.py itself follows by importing completeness.py for
literal extraction. Two records of one fact drift; they always have.

What this tool owns is the MECHANICS: sequencing the proof, running it in the
right tree state, and refusing to emit GO when a step was skipped.

THE CHECKS, and the specific failure each one is scar tissue from
-----------------------------------------------------------------
  worktree          scaffold present and is a real git worktree
  baseline-clean    tree has no tracked modifications -- a both-ways proof run
                    against a dirty tree proves nothing, and a reference impl
                    left applied hands the model the answer
  task-shape        TASK.md carries the sections a spec-complete task needs
  task-unchanged    TASK.md is byte-identical to the last NO-GO run. THE BIG
                    ONE. An edit you believe landed but did not gets read as
                    model failure for as many rounds as you allow; the entire
                    5-round automerge arc was one requirement whose edit never
                    reached the file. Costs nothing and catches it every time.
  required-literals every --require literal is actually IN TASK.md
  heredoc-escaping  an unquoted heredoc delimiter feeding an interpreter lets
                    the shell eat `$` and backticks in the source -- hit 3x in
                    one session, each time it read as the model writing bad code
  env-parity        the baseline run did not die on ImportError / missing tool.
                    A verify that cannot import the target fails for a reason
                    that has nothing to do with the task, on every iteration.
  spec-gap          every env var a fixture READS is NAMED in TASK.md. A var the
                    fixture sets but the task never names makes the model
                    hardcode a path and every check dies pre-logic.
  target-parses     the file the model is told to edit parses right now
  verify-shape      verify.sh uses the counting idiom and can report a FAIL
                    (not `set -e`, which aborts before printing anything)
  baseline-fails    BOTH-WAYS, half 1: verify MUST fail on the untouched tree,
                    else it certifies a run that changed nothing
  refimpl-passes    BOTH-WAYS, half 2: a reference impl MUST make it print
                    VERIFY_OK, else the task is unsatisfiable as specified
  refimpl-satisfies with the refimpl applied, every "Must contain" literal is in
                    the CHANGED/TARGET files -- scanning the whole tree makes
                    this inert in any real repo. If verify goes green WITHOUT
                    them, the verify does
                    not enforce the spec -- that is a benign/vacuous verify
                    caught mechanically, before it passes broken work.
  verify-relevance  MUTATION RELEVANCE. With the refimpl applied, perturb the
                    lines it added (flip a comparison, negate a branch, drop a
                    statement, nudge a constant) and ask whether the verify
                    goes red. A verify that tests a PROXY -- a grep, an AST
                    shape, a benign case -- lets the mutants pass while the
                    property is broken. Delegated to verify-relevance.py.
                    A HARD NO-GO on `low`: a verify that lets property-breaking
                    mutants of the reference impl survive cannot certify a fix,
                    so a low score STOPS the dispatch outright (it is not merely
                    human-gated -- signoff.py also reads it from the preflight
                    ledger and refuses to auto-approve). UNPROVEN -- never a
                    pass -- when it could not measure; a --no-relevance waiver
                    keeps the dispatch human-gated but cannot auto-approve.
  refimpl-reverted  the tree is back at baseline, so the model does not start
                    from the answer
  verify-quality    delegated to verify-quality.py
  verify-speed      verify is fast enough to run every iteration

Exit codes: 0 = GO, 1 = NO-GO, 2 = usage/environment error.

  ollama-dispatch-preflight <worktree> --refimpl fix.patch
  ollama-dispatch-preflight <worktree> --refimpl-cmd 'python3 apply_fix.py' --json
  ollama-dispatch-preflight <worktree> --no-refimpl   # explicit, logged waiver
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import ast
import hashlib
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path

BIN = Path(__file__).resolve().parent

# TS/JS syntax check -- the ast.parse analogue for a .ts/.tsx/.js target. Without
# it a TS syntax error slips past target-parses (which only knew ast.parse).
TS_PARSE = BIN / "ts-mutator" / "ts-parse.mjs"
TS_SUFFIXES = (".ts", ".tsx", ".js", ".jsx", ".mjs", ".cjs")
# Suffixes a backticked scope-line token must carry to be inferred as a
# CREATION target that does not exist at baseline (papercut #5). Kept to real
# source extensions so prose like `is_safe()` or `THRESHOLD * 2` is never taken
# for a filename.
SRC_SUFFIXES = (".py", ".pyi", ".mts", ".cts", ".swift", ".go", ".rs",
                ".rb", ".java", ".kt", ".c", ".h", ".cpp", ".hpp",
                ".sh", ".sql") + TS_SUFFIXES
# The gate's OWN vendored tsx (bin/ts-mutator, `npm install -D tsx`). The gate
# already trusts and installs bin/ts-mutator for the TS AST mutator; tsx rides
# the same install so a fresh checkout re-obtains it with `npm ci`.
TSX_VENDOR = BIN / "ts-mutator" / "node_modules" / ".bin" / "tsx"


def resolve_tsx(wt):
    """Resolve a runnable `tsx` command WITHOUT a silent network fetch.

    Returns (cmd_prefix_list, source) when tsx is resolvable, else (None, why).
    The old toolchain floor only checked the worktree's node_modules/.bin/tsx
    and, missing that, told the user verify would fall back to `npx --yes tsx`
    -- a network call that HANGS/FAILS on the offline queue host. Resolution
    order, all local:
      1. worktree node_modules/.bin/tsx  (the repo's own dev dep)
      2. the gate's vendored bin/ts-mutator/node_modules/.bin/tsx
      3. a `tsx` already on PATH
      4. `npx --offline tsx` ONLY when a cached tsx makes an offline run
         plausible (`--offline` forbids any fetch, so it fails fast instead of
         hanging); never a bare networked `npx --yes tsx`.
    When none resolve, return None + an honest cause so the caller NO-GOs with a
    named reason rather than promising a network fallback that cannot succeed.
    """
    if wt is not None:
        local = Path(wt) / "node_modules" / ".bin" / "tsx"
        if local.exists():
            return [str(local)], "worktree node_modules/.bin/tsx"
    if TSX_VENDOR.exists():
        return [str(TSX_VENDOR)], "gate vendored bin/ts-mutator/node_modules/.bin/tsx"
    onpath = shutil.which("tsx")
    if onpath:
        return [onpath], "tsx on PATH"
    if shutil.which("npx"):
        import glob as _glob
        cache = Path.home() / ".npm" / "_npx"
        if _glob.glob(str(cache / "*" / "node_modules" / ".bin" / "tsx")):
            return ["npx", "--offline", "tsx"], "npx --offline tsx (cached)"
    return None, ("tsx not resolvable locally and no network -- install tsx "
                  "(npm install -D tsx in the repo, or `cd ~/bin/ts-mutator && "
                  "npm install` for the gate's vendored copy)")
STATE_FILE = ".preflight-state.json"
# Overridable so the self-check can point at a fixture queue rather than
# the live one -- a test that mutates the real queue state is not a test.
QUEUE_STATE = Path(os.environ.get(
    "OLLAMA_QUEUE_STATE", Path.home() / "bin" / "ollama-queue-state.json"))
# A job in any of these states may touch its worktree at any moment.
ACTIVE_JOB_STATUSES = {"running", "pending", "paused"}
# ...and every state that certainly cannot. This second list is what makes the
# first one safe: it is a POLICY enumeration, not a proxy for something
# computable, so the failure mode to avoid is silent exclusion. A status the
# queue gains later belongs to neither set, and an unknown status must be
# reported rather than quietly treated as inactive -- "unrecognised status X"
# is recoverable, a collision the gate did not look for is not.
INACTIVE_JOB_STATUSES = {"done", "failed", "cancelled"}

# Blockers a TASK.md edit can plausibly clear. `task-unchanged` fires only when
# the previous NO-GO named one of these: a NO-GO for a dirty tree or a broken
# verify is CORRECTLY followed by an unchanged task, and firing there put a
# false "your edit did not land" at the top of the verdict -- above the real
# reason -- which trains you to ignore the one check that must never be ignored.
# NOT "task-unchanged" itself. Including it made the check SELF-SUSTAINING:
# once it fired, its own id was the prior blocker, so it fired again, and the
# only escape was editing TASK.md even when TASK.md was never the problem.
# A genuinely unfixed task blocker persists on its own -- required-literals
# stays red until the literal is there -- so nothing is lost by dropping it.
TASK_FIXABLE_BLOCKERS = {
    "task-shape", "required-literals", "spec-gap",
    "refimpl-passes", "refimpl-satisfies",
}
# Marker printed by a scaffold from `ollama-dispatch-scaffold` whose
# adversarial cases have not been authored yet. One-way, documented
# coupling: it lets the NO-GO name the real cause instead of the
# downstream verify-quality symptom.
SCAFFOLD_MARK = "SCAFFOLD_INCOMPLETE"
# Written into a fixture by `ollama-dispatch-draft` and removed only by its
# --confirm. A DRAFTED case is not an AUTHORED one: the gate can prove a
# verify discriminates, but not that it tests the RIGHT property, so the
# human read stays mandatory and is enforced here rather than trusted.
DRAFT_MARK = "DRAFT_UNCONFIRMED = True"
# The JS/TS analogue, written by `ollama-dispatch-draft` into a .mjs/.mts/.js
# fixture as `const DRAFT_UNCONFIRMED = true` (bare, or `;`-suffixed in the .mts
# symbol harness) and stripped only by its --confirm. Matched with a
# whitespace-tolerant regex so both emitted forms count -- without this the
# draft-confirmed gate was Python-only and an unconfirmed JS/TS draft reached GO
# without the human relevance review.
DRAFT_MARK_JS_RE = re.compile(r"const\s+DRAFT_UNCONFIRMED\s*=\s*true")
SLOW_VERIFY_S = 300          # skill: a verify runs every iteration; keep it fast
VERIFY_TIMEOUT_S = 900

# Ambient vars every process has. Not obligations on the spec.
AMBIENT = {
    "PATH", "HOME", "PWD", "OLDPWD", "SHELL", "USER", "LOGNAME", "TMPDIR",
    "LANG", "LC_ALL", "TERM", "PYTHONPATH", "PYTHONHASHSEED", "VIRTUAL_ENV",
    "PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE", "PYTHONUNBUFFERED", "CI", "TZ",
}

# The PREFLIGHT LEDGER: a copy of the gate's evidence written OUTSIDE the
# worktree. .preflight-state.json lives inside the tree the model edits, so
# anything downstream that trusts it for an autonomous decision is trusting a
# file the job could have rewritten. signoff.py reads relevance evidence from
# here only, keyed by the worktree path, and never from the in-tree copy.
PREFLIGHT_LEDGER = Path(os.environ.get(
    "OLLAMA_PREFLIGHT_LEDGER",
    Path.home() / "bin" / "ollama-queue-logs" / "preflight-ledger"))

PASS, FAIL, WARN, SKIP, UNPROVEN = "PASS", "FAIL", "WARN", "SKIP", "UNPR"
# UNPROVEN is NOT a pass and NOT an N/A. It means "this property was never
# established" -- the tool was missing, the evidence was absent, the step was
# waived. It exists because of a failure the Research session hit the same day
# this was written: a safety condition that read a key which did not exist
# evaluated to None, None is falsy, and a gameable verify rendered as a
# condition SATISFIED. An inert check is worse than no check, because it looks
# like a check that passed. So missing evidence gets its own loud outcome and
# is named individually on the GO line.


def _norm_rel(rel):
    """Normalise a relative token WITHOUT eating a hidden file's leading dot.

    `str.lstrip("./")` strips every leading char in the set {'.', '/'}, so
    `./.hidden.py` collapsed to `hidden.py` and the wrong file was hashed /
    parsed. Strip a leading `./` prefix (repeatedly) and any leading slash --
    but leave `.hidden.py` intact.
    """
    rel = (rel or "").strip()
    while rel.startswith("./"):
        rel = rel[2:]
    return rel.lstrip("/")


class Result:
    def __init__(self, cid, status, msg, detail="", fix=""):
        self.id, self.status, self.msg, self.detail, self.fix = (
            cid, status, msg, detail, fix)

    def as_dict(self):
        return {"check": self.id, "status": self.status, "message": self.msg,
                "detail": self.detail, "fix": self.fix}


class Preflight:
    def __init__(self, args):
        self.a = args
        self.wt = Path(args.worktree).expanduser().resolve()
        self.results: list[Result] = []
        self.task_text = ""
        self.verify_text = ""
        self.baseline_out = ""
        self.baseline_rc = None
        self.verify_secs = None
        self.follower = ""
        self.untracked_before: set[str] = set()
        self._state: dict = {}

    # ---- plumbing ------------------------------------------------------
    def add(self, cid, status, msg, detail="", fix=""):
        self.results.append(Result(cid, status, msg, detail, fix))
        return status

    def skipped(self, cid, why):
        """Genuinely not applicable -- there was nothing here to check."""
        return self.add(cid, SKIP, why)

    def unproven(self, cid, why, fix=""):
        """Applicable, but never established. Named loudly on the verdict line."""
        return self.add(cid, UNPROVEN, why, fix=fix)

    def load_state(self, path, require_scope=False):
        """Parse a preflight state / ledger JSON file into a dict.

        Shared by check_task_unchanged and run_review so the acceptance rule is
        applied in ONE place. Returns {} when the file is absent, unreadable,
        or not a JSON object; and, when require_scope is set, also {} unless it
        carries a `declared_scope`. That last rule is what stops a non-empty but
        contract-less in-tree .preflight-state.json from SHADOWING a real
        contract in --review -- the integrator (resolve_contract) already
        requires declared_scope before it will trust a state file, and this
        matches it.
        """
        try:
            d = json.loads(Path(path).read_text())
        except Exception:
            return {}
        if not isinstance(d, dict):
            return {}
        if require_scope and d.get("declared_scope") is None:
            return {}
        return d

    def _rel_to_wt(self, f):
        """Best-effort worktree-relative path. `relative_to` raises on an
        absolute --target outside the worktree; relpath does not, so one bad
        --target no longer takes declared_scope / scaffold_sha256 down with it
        (LOW8)."""
        try:
            return str(Path(f).relative_to(self.wt))
        except ValueError:
            return os.path.relpath(str(f), str(self.wt))

    def _prior_contract(self):
        """The last recorded scope contract (baseline_commit / declared_scope /
        scaffold_sha256), from the ledger (authoritative, outside the tree) or
        the in-tree state, whichever carries one.

        Consulted by report() to CARRY THE CONTRACT FORWARD on a run that did
        not reach a clean baseline, so a NO-GO on a tampered tree never rewrites
        the good contract with hashes of the already-modified files (HIGH1).
        """
        for src in (self.ledger_path_for(self.wt), self.wt / STATE_FILE):
            d = self.load_state(src)
            if d.get("declared_scope") is not None or d.get("scaffold_sha256"):
                return d
        return {}

    def git(self, *a, check=False):
        p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(self.wt), *a],
                           capture_output=True, text=True)
        if check and p.returncode != 0:
            raise RuntimeError(f"git {' '.join(a)}: {p.stderr.strip()}")
        return p.returncode, p.stdout, p.stderr

    def sh(self, cmd, timeout=VERIFY_TIMEOUT_S, env=None):
        """Run a shell command in the worktree. Returns (rc, combined output).

        Deliberately NOT inheriting this process's env wholesale beyond os.environ:
        the verify is supposed to set what it needs, and a var leaking in from the
        coordinator's shell is precisely how a spec gap hides.
        """
        e = dict(os.environ)
        if env:
            e.update(env)
        try:
            p = subprocess.run(cmd, shell=True, cwd=str(self.wt), env=e,
                               capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
            return p.returncode, (p.stdout or "") + (p.stderr or "")
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            return 124, f"TIMEOUT after {timeout}s"

    # ---- transitive verify text (delegated, not reimplemented) ---------
    def _load_verify_quality(self):
        f = BIN / "verify-quality.py"
        if not f.exists():
            return None
        try:
            sp = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("verify_quality", str(f))
            m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(sp)
            sp.loader.exec_module(m)
            return m
        except Exception:
            return None

    def collect_verify_text(self):
        """The verify command PLUS every script it transitively runs.

        Reuses verify-quality.py's follower. Scanning only the `bash verify.sh`
        string is the wrong-artifact bug that made every text check in
        verify-quality.py fire spuriously until it was fixed there; inheriting
        the fix is the whole point of delegating.
        """
        vq = self._load_verify_quality()
        if vq and hasattr(vq, "verify_text"):
            try:
                self.follower = "verify-quality.py (transitive)"
                return vq.verify_text(self.a.verify, self.wt)
            except Exception:
                pass
        self.follower = ("built-in fallback (one hop) -- verify-quality.py's "
                         "follower was unavailable, so the text checks below "
                         "see less than they should")
        # Fallback: verify.sh plus one hop of obvious python it runs.
        text = self.a.verify
        vf = self.wt / "verify.sh"
        if vf.exists():
            text += "\n" + vf.read_text(errors="replace")
        for m in re.finditer(r"[\w./-]+\.py", text):
            f = self.wt / m.group(0)
            if f.is_file() and f.stat().st_size < 400_000:
                text += "\n" + f.read_text(errors="replace")
        return text

    def followed_py_files(self):
        """The .py files the verify actually runs -- the fixtures."""
        out, seen = [], set()
        text = self.a.verify
        vf = self.wt / "verify.sh"
        if vf.exists():
            text += "\n" + vf.read_text(errors="replace")
        for m in re.finditer(r"([\w./-]+\.py)\b", text):
            rel = m.group(1)
            if rel in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(rel)
            f = self.wt / _norm_rel(rel)
            if f.is_file() and f.stat().st_size < 400_000:
                out.append(f)
        return out

    # ---- static checks -------------------------------------------------
    def check_worktree(self):
        if not self.wt.is_dir():
            return self.add("worktree", FAIL, f"{self.wt} is not a directory")
        missing = [n for n in ("TASK.md", "verify.sh") if not (self.wt / n).exists()]
        # honour a non-default --task-file
        tf = self.wt / self.a.task_file
        if self.a.task_file != "TASK.md":
            missing = [m for m in missing if m != "TASK.md"]
            if not tf.exists():
                missing.append(self.a.task_file)
        if missing:
            return self.add("worktree", FAIL,
                            f"missing scaffold: {', '.join(missing)}",
                            fix="see ollama-dispatch skill steps 2-3")
        rc, out, _ = self.git("rev-parse", "--git-dir")
        if rc != 0:
            return self.add("worktree", FAIL, f"{self.wt} is not a git repo",
                            fix="git worktree add <scratch>/<label> -b fix/<label>")
        is_wt = "worktrees" in out
        self.is_wt = is_wt  # a linked `git worktree` (dispatch flow), not a clone
        rc, br, _ = self.git("rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD")
        self.task_text = tf.read_text(errors="replace")
        self.verify_text = self.collect_verify_text()
        if self.follower.startswith("built-in"):
            # Say so out loud. A text check running against a smaller haystack
            # than it thinks it has produces confident nonsense -- the exact
            # wrong-artifact bug verify-quality.py had to fix in itself.
            self.add("verify-follower", WARN,
                     "using the built-in one-hop script follower",
                     detail=self.follower)
        return self.add("worktree", PASS,
                        f"scaffold present on branch {br.strip() or '?'}"
                        + ("" if is_wt else " (a clone, not a `git worktree` -- "
                                            "fine, but isolation is on you)"))

    def check_cwd_exclusive(self):
        """No other job may already own this worktree.

        Two separate hazards, and the second is the one that bites silently.

        (a) The obvious one: a second job starting in a tree that already
            carries a first job's edits verifies against a baseline that is not
            a baseline. Its verify can pass vacuously, and on two hosts they
            race on the same files mid-write.

        (b) THIS GATE IS DESTRUCTIVE. It runs the verify, APPLIES A REFERENCE
            IMPL, and then `git checkout -- .` to revert. `baseline-clean`
            protects a job that has already edited something -- a dirty tree
            short-circuits before any of that. But a job in its first iteration
            has not written yet, so the tree is clean, the gate proceeds, and
            for the length of the refimpl run that job is looking at A TREE
            CONTAINING THE ANSWER. It can read the reference impl, "solve" the
            task, and produce a green verify that proves nothing about the
            model. That is worse than corruption, because it looks like success.

        So this runs BEFORE baseline-clean and hard-stops. An unreachable queue
        state file is UNPROVEN, not PASS -- the collision is unmeasured, not
        absent.
        """
        try:
            raw = QUEUE_STATE.read_text()
        except Exception as e:
            return self.unproven(
                "cwd-exclusive",
                f"queue state unreadable ({type(e).__name__}) -- cannot tell "
                "whether another job already owns this tree",
                fix=f"check {QUEUE_STATE}; a second job in one worktree "
                    "verifies against a tree that is not at baseline")
        try:
            jobs = json.loads(raw).get("jobs") or []
            if isinstance(jobs, dict):
                jobs = list(jobs.values())
        except Exception:
            return self.unproven("cwd-exclusive",
                                 "queue state is not readable JSON")
        mine = self.wt.resolve()
        clash = []
        unknown = set()
        for j in jobs:
            if not isinstance(j, dict):
                # A malformed queue entry (a bare string, null) must not crash
                # the gate -- `j.get` on a non-dict raises. Skip it; a job we
                # cannot read cannot be shown to own this tree.
                continue
            st = j.get("status")
            if st in INACTIVE_JOB_STATUSES:
                continue
            if st not in ACTIVE_JOB_STATUSES:
                # Neither known-active nor known-inactive: treat as active AND
                # say so, rather than silently skipping a job that may hold
                # this tree.
                unknown.add(str(st))
            raw_cwd = j.get("cwd")
            if not raw_cwd:
                continue
            try:
                other = Path(raw_cwd).resolve()
            except Exception:
                continue
            # Equal, or one nested inside the other -- a worktree inside another
            # job's worktree collides just as hard as sharing the path.
            if other == mine or other in mine.parents or mine in other.parents:
                clash.append(f"{str(j.get('id') or '?')[:12]} [{j.get('status')}] "
                             f"{j.get('label') or ''} -> {other}")
        if clash:
            return self.add(
                "cwd-exclusive", FAIL,
                f"{len(clash)} active job(s) already own this worktree",
                detail="; ".join(clash),
                fix="give this dispatch its OWN worktree ("
                    "`git worktree add <new> HEAD`). Do not run this gate "
                    "against a tree an active job holds -- it applies a "
                    "reference impl, which that job could read as the answer.")
        if unknown:
            return self.add(
                "cwd-exclusive", WARN,
                f"no active job owns this worktree, but {len(unknown)} job(s) "
                "carry a status this gate does not recognise",
                detail=", ".join(sorted(unknown)),
                fix="add the status to ACTIVE_JOB_STATUSES or "
                    "INACTIVE_JOB_STATUSES in ollama-dispatch-preflight -- an "
                    "unclassified status was counted as active for this run")
        return self.add("cwd-exclusive", PASS,
                        "no active job owns this worktree")

    def check_baseline_clean(self):
        rc, out, _ = self.git("status", "--porcelain")
        dirty = []
        self.untracked_before = set()
        for line in out.splitlines():
            if not line.strip():
                continue
            code, path = line[:2], line[3:].strip()
            if code == "??":
                self.untracked_before.add(path)
            else:
                dirty.append(f"{code.strip()} {path}")
        if dirty:
            return self.add(
                "baseline-clean", FAIL,
                f"{len(dirty)} tracked file(s) already modified: "
                + ", ".join(dirty[:5]),
                detail="The both-ways proof is meaningless on a dirty tree, and a "
                       "reference impl left applied hands the model the answer.",
                fix="git -C %s checkout -- ." % self.wt)
        return self.add("baseline-clean", PASS,
                        f"no tracked modifications ({len(self.untracked_before)} "
                        f"untracked scaffold file(s))")

    # Harness files `--seal-baseline` commits into the launch baseline. Kept in
    # sync with seal_baseline() in ollama-dispatch-scaffold. refimpl.py / fix.patch
    # are the reference impl -- NOT the harness -- and must stay out.
    HARNESS_FILES = ("TASK.md", "verify.sh", "verify_impl.mts", "verify_impl.mjs",
                     "verify_impl.js", "test_fixture.py", "check_literals.py",
                     "refimpl.py", "Package.swift")

    def check_harness_sealed(self):
        """Fold `--seal-baseline` into the GO decision (gap #2).

        The authored harness (TASK.md, verify.sh, the fixture, check_literals.py)
        must be TRACKED and UNMODIFIED at launch, or the queue records a DIRTY
        launch baseline and the scope gate flags the harness riding into the
        model's diff -- the exact frictions `--seal-baseline` exists to remove.
        Sealing was a separate step a human had to remember to run last; this
        check makes forgetting it a loud NO-GO (with the exact seal command), or
        seals it here when `--auto-seal` is passed.

        Scoped to a real linked `git worktree` -- the dispatch flow. A plain
        clone or `git init` tree ("isolation is on you", and the shape the gate's
        own self-test builds) is NOT subject to it: there is no queue launch
        baseline to keep clean, and forcing a commit there would be surprising.
        """
        if not getattr(self, "is_wt", False):
            return  # not the dispatch worktree flow; nothing to seal
        # A harness file is un-sealed if it is untracked (??) or tracked but
        # modified vs HEAD. Either way it would dirty the launch baseline.
        present = [h for h in self.HARNESS_FILES if (self.wt / h).is_file()]
        untracked = [h for h in present if h in self.untracked_before]
        rc, out, _ = self.git("status", "--porcelain", "--", *present) if present else (0, "", "")
        modified = []
        for line in out.splitlines():
            code, path = line[:2], line[3:].strip()
            if code != "??" and path in present:
                modified.append(path)
        unsealed = sorted(set(untracked) | set(modified))
        if not unsealed:
            return self.add("harness-sealed", PASS,
                            "authored harness is tracked and unmodified")
        seal_cmd = f"ollama-dispatch-scaffold --seal-baseline {self.wt}"
        if getattr(self.a, "auto_seal", False):
            rc, out, err = self.git("add", "--", *unsealed)
            if rc == 0:
                rc, out, err = self.git(
                    "commit", "-qm", "seal dispatch harness (clean launch baseline)")
            if rc == 0:
                # refresh the untracked set so downstream checks see the tree as
                # it now stands.
                self.untracked_before -= set(unsealed)
                return self.add("harness-sealed", PASS,
                                "auto-sealed the harness into the baseline: "
                                + ", ".join(unsealed),
                                detail="launch tree is now clean; the harness is "
                                       "tracked and will not ride into the diff")
            return self.add("harness-sealed", FAIL,
                            "--auto-seal could not commit the harness",
                            detail=(err or out).strip()[-300:], fix=seal_cmd)
        return self.add(
            "harness-sealed", FAIL,
            f"{len(unsealed)} harness file(s) not sealed into the baseline: "
            + ", ".join(unsealed),
            detail="An un-sealed harness makes the queue record a DIRTY launch "
                   "baseline (the diff is not attributable to the job) and the "
                   "scope gate flag the fixture riding into `git diff HEAD`. "
                   "Seal it (or re-run this gate with --auto-seal).",
            fix=seal_cmd)

    def check_toolchain(self):
        """Assert the toolchain a TS/JS dispatch actually needs (gap #3).

        Two soft floors bit real dispatches: `tsx` missing from a repo's
        node_modules/.bin (only tsc present) fell back to a network `npx` that
        fails on the offline queue host; and bin/ts-mutator having no installed
        `typescript` silently degraded the ts-parse floor AND left
        verify-relevance unable to make TS AST-span mutants (so a one-hunk TS fix
        -- the norm -- comes back UNPROVEN, which reads as a mystery). This turns
        the floors a given mode REQUIRES into a NO-GO with the fix command, while
        still WARNing on a tool that is genuinely optional for that mode.

        Python / Swift targets need none of this -- no result is added for them.
        """
        targets = [t for t in self.target_files() if t.suffix in TS_SUFFIXES]
        if not targets:
            return  # not a TS/JS dispatch; nothing to assert
        verify_src = (self.wt / "verify.sh").read_text(errors="replace") \
            if (self.wt / "verify.sh").is_file() else ""
        nm_bin = self.wt / "node_modules" / ".bin"
        problems, notes = [], []

        # node: required for every TS/JS mode (fixture runner AND the mutator).
        if not shutil.which("node"):
            problems.append("node is not on PATH (needed to run the TS fixture "
                            "and the relevance mutator)")

        # tsc: the authoritative type gate -- required whenever the verify runs it.
        if "tsc" in verify_src:
            if not (nm_bin / "tsc").exists() and not shutil.which("npx"):
                problems.append("tsc is not resolvable: no node_modules/.bin/tsc "
                                "and no npx to fall back to")

        # tsx: only the SYMBOL harness imports a typed module under tsx. The
        # expression harness runs plain node, so tsx is genuinely optional there.
        # Match tsx as an INVOCATION (assignment / $TSX / a bin path / npx tsx),
        # never a bare mention in a comment ("no tsx needed" must not trip this).
        verify_uses_tsx = bool(re.search(
            r"TSX=|\$TSX|\.bin/tsx|npx\s+(?:--yes\s+)?tsx", verify_src))
        if verify_uses_tsx:
            tsx_cmd, tsx_src = resolve_tsx(self.wt)
            if tsx_cmd is None:
                # HONEST NO-GO: do not promise a network `npx --yes tsx` that
                # hangs/fails on the offline queue host. tsx is genuinely
                # unresolvable here; name the cause and the fix.
                problems.append(
                    "tsx is required by verify.sh but " + tsx_src
                    + " (fix: npm install -D tsx in the repo, or `cd "
                    + str(BIN / "ts-mutator") + " && npm install` for the "
                    "gate's vendored copy)")
            elif tsx_src.startswith("npx"):
                # Resolvable only via an offline-capable npx cache -- usable,
                # but flag it: not the repo's own dep.
                notes.append("tsx resolved via " + tsx_src + " (offline cache "
                             "hit, no network); prefer `npm install -D tsx` in "
                             "the repo.")
            # else: resolved to a concrete local binary -> no caveat.

        # ts-mutator typescript: REQUIRED, because verify-relevance (a hard gate)
        # needs it to make AST-span mutants for a TS target, and a one-hunk TS fix
        # yields no hunk-revert evidence -- so without it relevance is UNPROVEN
        # and the dispatch cannot pass. The ts-parse floor in verify.sh shares the
        # install and degrades to WARN on its own; this NO-GO is about relevance.
        tsm_ts = BIN / "ts-mutator" / "node_modules" / "typescript"
        if not tsm_ts.exists():
            problems.append(
                "bin/ts-mutator has no installed `typescript`, so verify-relevance "
                "cannot generate TS mutants and a single-hunk TS fix comes back "
                "UNPROVEN (fix: (cd " + str(BIN / "ts-mutator") + " && npm install))")

        if problems:
            return self.add(
                "toolchain", FAIL,
                f"{len(problems)} required TS/JS tool(s) missing",
                detail="; ".join(problems + notes),
                fix=problems[0].split("(fix: ")[-1].rstrip(")")
                    if "(fix: " in problems[0] else "install the missing tool")
        if notes:
            return self.add("toolchain", WARN,
                            "TS/JS toolchain usable with a caveat",
                            detail="; ".join(notes))
        return self.add("toolchain", PASS,
                        "TS/JS toolchain present (node, tsc, ts-mutator typescript)")

    def check_task_shape(self):
        """Mechanical presence of the sections a spec-complete task needs.

        Presence is decidable; adequacy is not. Reported as WARN rather than a
        blocker for everything except an empty spec -- a gate that cries wolf
        gets ignored, and then every check it makes stops counting.
        """
        t = self.task_text
        if len(t.strip()) < 200:
            return self.add("task-shape", FAIL,
                            f"{self.a.task_file} is {len(t.strip())} chars -- "
                            "not a spec")
        missing = []
        if not re.search(r"confirmed|reproduc|observed", t, re.I):
            missing.append("a CONFIRMED defect (symptom + how you verified it, "
                           "not a suspected mechanism)")
        if not re.search(r"[\w/.-]+\.\w+:\d+|line\s+~?\d+", t):
            missing.append("an entry point as file:line")
        if not re.search(r"must contain", t, re.I):
            missing.append('a "Must contain" list of literal tokens')
        if not re.search(r"verify\.sh", t):
            missing.append("the loop instruction (run `bash verify.sh` after "
                           "every edit until VERIFY_OK)")
        if not re.search(r"only edit|do not edit|don't edit", t, re.I):
            missing.append('a scope line ("Only edit `<file>`; do not edit '
                           'verify.sh")')
        if missing:
            return self.add("task-shape", WARN,
                            f"{len(missing)} spec element(s) not found",
                            detail="; ".join(missing),
                            fix="ollama-dispatch skill step 2")
        return self.add("task-shape", PASS, "carries all 5 spec-complete elements")

    def check_task_unchanged(self):
        """Did your last edit to TASK.md actually land?

        This outranks every other check. An edit you believe landed but did not
        is indistinguishable, from the outside, from a model that cannot do the
        task -- and it stays indistinguishable for as many rounds as you allow.
        """
        sf = self.wt / STATE_FILE
        digest = hashlib.sha256(self.task_text.encode()).hexdigest()
        # No require_scope here: task-unchanged must still read task_sha256 from
        # a prior NO-GO that never recorded a contract (a dirty-baseline run).
        prev = self.load_state(sf)
        self._state.update({"task_sha256": digest, "task_file": self.a.task_file,
                            "at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S")})
        prev_blockers = set(prev.get("blockers") or []) if prev else set()
        task_fixable = prev_blockers & TASK_FIXABLE_BLOCKERS
        # ...but if any OTHER scaffold file changed since the last run, an
        # unchanged TASK.md is exactly right. The verify, the fixture and the
        # frozen literal list are all legitimate places to fix a NO-GO that
        # TASK_FIXABLE_BLOCKERS attributes to the task -- e.g. verify-quality,
        # cleared by re-freezing check_literals.py. Firing there reproduces the
        # false "your edit did not land" this check was already narrowed once
        # to avoid.
        scaffold_moved = False
        try:
            prev_sc = prev.get("scaffold_sha256") if prev else None
            if prev_sc:
                now_sc = self.scaffold_digests()
                tf = self.a.task_file
                scaffold_moved = ({k: v for k, v in now_sc.items() if k != tf}
                                  != {k: v for k, v in prev_sc.items()
                                      if k != tf})
        except Exception:
            scaffold_moved = False
        if (prev and prev.get("task_sha256") == digest
                and prev.get("verdict") == "NO-GO" and task_fixable
                and not scaffold_moved):
            return self.add(
                "task-unchanged", FAIL,
                f"{self.a.task_file} is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the last run, which "
                "was a NO-GO",
                detail="You edited the task and re-ran; the file did not change. "
                       "The edit did NOT land. Do not enqueue -- the next round "
                       "would fail for the same reason and read as model "
                       "incapacity.",
                fix=f"re-apply the edit, then: grep -c '<your literal>' "
                    f"{self.wt / self.a.task_file}")
        if prev and prev.get("task_sha256") == digest:
            if prev.get("verdict") == "NO-GO":
                why = ("the scaffold changed instead" if scaffold_moved else
                       "the last NO-GO was not the task's fault "
                       f"({', '.join(sorted(prev_blockers)) or 'no blockers'})")
                return self.add("task-unchanged", PASS,
                                f"unchanged, and {why}")
            return self.add("task-unchanged", PASS,
                            "unchanged since the last run (which was a GO)")
        return self.add("task-unchanged", PASS,
                        "changed since the last run"
                        if prev else "first run on this task")

    def check_required_literals(self):
        reqs = list(self.a.require or [])
        if self.a.require_file:
            p = Path(self.a.require_file).expanduser()
            if not p.exists():
                return self.add("required-literals", FAIL,
                                f"--require-file {p} does not exist")
            reqs += [l.rstrip("\n") for l in p.read_text().splitlines()
                     if l.strip() and not l.lstrip().startswith("#")]
        if not reqs:
            return self.unproven(
                "required-literals",
                "no --require given -- the edit-landed guard is OFF",
                fix="pass the literal your last edit added: "
                    "--require 'def should_hard_delete_on_upgrade(' . An edit "
                    "you believe landed but did not is read as model failure "
                    "for as many rounds as you allow.")
        absent = [r for r in reqs if r not in self.task_text]
        if absent:
            return self.add("required-literals", FAIL,
                            f"{len(absent)} required literal(s) NOT in "
                            f"{self.a.task_file}",
                            detail="; ".join(repr(r) for r in absent[:5]),
                            fix="EDIT DID NOT LAND -- re-apply it")
        return self.add("required-literals", PASS,
                        f"all {len(reqs)} required literal(s) present")

    def check_heredoc_escaping(self):
        """An UNQUOTED heredoc delimiter lets the shell expand the body.

        `python3 - <<PY` runs the body through shell expansion first, so a `$` or
        a backtick in the python source is eaten before python ever sees it --
        and the resulting SyntaxError reads as the model writing broken code.
        `<<'PY'` disables expansion; chr() codes avoid the problem entirely for
        content that must contain shell-special characters.
        """
        vf = self.wt / "verify.sh"
        files = [vf] if vf.exists() else []
        files += [p for p in self.wt.glob("*.sh") if p != vf]
        if not files:
            return self.unproven("heredoc-escaping",
                                 "no shell script found to scan")
        problems, checked = [], 0
        for f in files:
            try:
                lines = f.read_text(errors="replace").splitlines()
            except Exception:
                continue
            i = 0
            while i < len(lines):
                line = lines[i]
                m = re.search(r"<<-?\s*(['\"]?)([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\1", line)
                if not m:
                    i += 1
                    continue
                quoted, delim = bool(m.group(1)), m.group(2)
                interp = re.search(r"\b(python3?|node|deno|ruby|perl)\b", line)
                body, j = [], i + 1
                while j < len(lines) and lines[j].strip() != delim:
                    body.append(lines[j])
                    j += 1
                checked += 1
                if not quoted and interp:
                    btext = "\n".join(body)
                    hits = []
                    if re.search(r"(?<!\\)\$", btext):
                        hits.append("$")
                    if "`" in btext:
                        hits.append("`")
                    if hits:
                        problems.append(
                            f"{f.name}:{i+1} `<<{delim}` is UNQUOTED and feeds "
                            f"{interp.group(1)}; the shell will expand "
                            f"{' and '.join(hits)} in the body")
                i = max(j, i + 1)
        # The eval'd-single-quoted-check idiom: `chk 'python3 - <<PY ...'`
        if vf.exists():
            vtext = vf.read_text(errors="replace")
            if re.search(r"\beval\s+[\"']?\$", vtext):
                for m in re.finditer(r"'([^']*<<[^']*)'", vtext, re.S):
                    frag = m.group(1)
                    bad = [c for c in ('\\"', "$") if c in frag]
                    if bad:
                        problems.append(
                            f"verify.sh: a single-quoted check body containing a "
                            f"heredoc is eval'd and contains "
                            f"{' and '.join(repr(b) for b in bad)} -- these break "
                            f"under eval (SyntaxError / shell expansion)")
        if problems:
            return self.add(
                "heredoc-escaping", FAIL,
                f"{len(problems)} heredoc/escaping hazard(s)",
                detail=" | ".join(problems[:4]),
                fix="quote the delimiter (<<'PY'), or build shell-special "
                    "content from chr() codes: chr(34)=\" chr(39)=' chr(36)=$ "
                    "chr(92)=backslash chr(10)=newline")
        return self.add("heredoc-escaping", PASS,
                        f"{checked} heredoc(s) safe in {len(files)} script(s)"
                        if checked
                        else f"no heredocs in {len(files)} script(s)")

    def task_names(self, var):
        """Does TASK.md name this env var AS ITS OWN TOKEN?

        A bare `var in self.task_text` passes on any SUPERSTRING: a task that
        mentions OLLAMA_QUEUE_LOG_DIR was read as naming LOG_DIR, so the model
        was never told about LOG_DIR and every iteration would die on a KeyError
        before reaching the logic. That is precisely the OLLAMA_QUEUE_LOGS-class
        miss this check exists to catch, and the check was passing it.

        Same shape as the whole-tree scan in refimpl-satisfies: a substring test
        looks discriminating on a fixture that happens to contain no confusable
        superstring, and fixtures never do by accident.
        """
        return re.search(r"(?<![A-Za-z0-9_])" + re.escape(var)
                         + r"(?![A-Za-z0-9_])", self.task_text) is not None

    def fixture_py_files(self):
        """followed_py_files() MINUS the declared target(s).

        verify.sh legitimately names the target -- a parse check
        (`ast.parse(open('thing.py'))`) is the standard first section, and the
        generator emits one. Counting it as a fixture makes the TARGET's own
        module-scope config look like a fixture dependency: on a config-heavy
        module that is 45 vars of pure noise.
        """
        targets = set()
        for f in self.target_files():
            try:
                targets.add(f.resolve())
            except Exception:
                pass
        return [f for f in self.followed_py_files()
                if f.resolve() not in targets]

    def followed_js_files(self):
        """The .mjs/.mts/.js/.ts fixtures the verify actually runs -- the JS/TS
        analogue of followed_py_files(). A JS/TS dispatch runs a verify_impl.mjs
        / verify_impl.mts (or .js/.ts) fixture, and those are exactly where a
        DRAFT_UNCONFIRMED marker lands, so draft-confirmed must be able to see
        them."""
        out, seen = [], set()
        text = self.a.verify
        vf = self.wt / "verify.sh"
        if vf.exists():
            text += "\n" + vf.read_text(errors="replace")
        for m in re.finditer(r"([\w./-]+\.(?:mjs|mts|js|ts|jsx|cjs))\b", text):
            rel = m.group(1)
            if rel in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(rel)
            f = self.wt / _norm_rel(rel)
            if f.is_file() and f.stat().st_size < 400_000:
                out.append(f)
        return out

    def fixture_js_files(self):
        """followed_js_files() MINUS the declared target(s) -- same exclusion as
        fixture_py_files(): the file the model EDITS is not one of its own
        fixtures."""
        targets = set()
        for f in self.target_files():
            try:
                targets.add(f.resolve())
            except Exception:
                pass
        return [f for f in self.followed_js_files()
                if f.resolve() not in targets]

    @staticmethod
    def _is_environ(node):
        """`os.environ` (or a bare `environ`)."""
        if isinstance(node, ast.Attribute) and node.attr == "environ":
            return True
        return isinstance(node, ast.Name) and node.id == "environ"

    def env_vars_in(self, src):
        """(required_reads, sets) -- decided from SYNTAX, not from a pattern.

        `os.environ.get(X, default)` CANNOT FAIL. Flagging it was a containment
        test standing in for the property: "appears in an environ expression" is
        not "is a dependency the model must be told about". The property is
        REQUIRED-ness, and it is decidable:

            os.environ[X]                     required   -> block
            os.environ.get(X) / getenv(X)     required   -> block
            os.environ.get(X, d) / getenv(X,d) defaulted -> ignore entirely
            os.environ[X] = ...               SET        -> block (this is the
                                                           OLLAMA_QUEUE_LOGS
                                                           miss: a var the
                                                           fixture establishes
                                                           and the task never
                                                           names)
        """
        required, sets = {}, {}
        try:
            tree = ast.parse(src)
        except SyntaxError:
            return required, sets

        def name_of(node):
            if isinstance(node, ast.Constant) and isinstance(node.value, str):
                return node.value
            return None

        for node in ast.walk(tree):
            if isinstance(node, ast.Subscript) and self._is_environ(node.value):
                v = name_of(node.slice)
                if not v:
                    continue
                (sets if isinstance(node.ctx, ast.Store) else required)[v] = True
            elif isinstance(node, ast.Call):
                fn = node.func
                v = name_of(node.args[0]) if node.args else None
                if not v:
                    continue
                if (isinstance(fn, ast.Attribute) and fn.attr == "get"
                        and self._is_environ(fn.value)):
                    if len(node.args) == 1:      # no default -> can KeyError
                        required[v] = True
                elif isinstance(fn, ast.Attribute) and fn.attr == "getenv":
                    if len(node.args) == 1:
                        required[v] = True
                elif isinstance(fn, ast.Name) and fn.id == "getenv":
                    if len(node.args) == 1:
                        required[v] = True
                elif (isinstance(fn, ast.Attribute) and fn.attr == "setdefault"
                        and self._is_environ(fn.value)):
                    sets[v] = True
        return required, sets

    def check_spec_gap(self):
        """Every env var a FIXTURE READS must be NAMED in the task.

        Split deliberately: a var a fixture *reads* is a contract the model has
        to honour and its absence from the spec kills every check pre-logic
        (BLOCK). A var verify.sh merely *exports* is usually import-time
        scaffolding the model never touches (WARN) -- blocking on those trains
        the coordinator to ignore the check, which costs more than it saves.
        """
        read_vars: dict[str, str] = {}
        fixtures = self.fixture_py_files()
        for f in fixtures:
            try:
                src = f.read_text(errors="replace")
            except Exception:
                continue
            required, sets = self.env_vars_in(src)
            for v in required:
                read_vars.setdefault(v, f"required in {f.name}")
            for v in sets:
                read_vars.setdefault(v, f"SET by {f.name}")
        exported = set()
        vf = self.wt / "verify.sh"
        if vf.exists():
            for m in re.finditer(r"^\s*export\s+([A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{2,})=",
                                 vf.read_text(errors="replace"), re.M):
                exported.add(m.group(1))
        gap_read = {v: src for v, src in read_vars.items()
                    if v not in AMBIENT and not self.task_names(v)}
        gap_exp = {v for v in exported
                   if v not in AMBIENT and not self.task_names(v)
                   and v not in read_vars}
        if gap_read:
            return self.add(
                "spec-gap", FAIL,
                f"{len(gap_read)} env var(s) a fixture REQUIRES or SETS but "
                f"{self.a.task_file} never names",
                detail="; ".join(f"${v} ({s})"
                                 for v, s in list(gap_read.items())[:5]),
                fix="name each var, and what it points at, in the task")
        if gap_exp:
            return self.add(
                "spec-gap", WARN,
                f"{len(gap_exp)} env var(s) exported by verify.sh and not named "
                f"in {self.a.task_file}",
                detail=", ".join(f"${v}" for v in sorted(gap_exp)[:8])
                       + " -- harmless if they are only import-time dummies",
                fix="name them in the task if the model's code must consult them")
        if not (read_vars or exported):
            # Distinguish "the fixtures read no env vars" from "I never found
            # the fixtures". The second is the wrong-artifact bug that made
            # every text check in verify-quality.py fire on an empty haystack.
            mentions_py = bool(re.search(r"[\w./-]+\.py\b", self.verify_text))
            if mentions_py and not self.followed_py_files():
                return self.unproven(
                    "spec-gap",
                    "the verify names .py files but none were found in the "
                    "worktree -- env vars were scanned in an EMPTY haystack",
                    fix="check the fixture paths in verify.sh resolve from "
                        f"{self.wt}")
            return self.add("spec-gap", PASS,
                            f"no REQUIRED env vars in "
                            f"{len(fixtures)} fixture file(s) "
                            "(defaulted reads cannot fail, so they are not "
                            "obligations on the spec)")
        return self.add("spec-gap", PASS,
                        f"all {len(read_vars) + len(exported)} fixture env "
                        f"var(s) named in the task")

    def target_files(self):
        """Files the task says to edit. Explicit --target wins."""
        if self.a.target:
            return [self.wt / t for t in self.a.target]
        out, seen = [], set()
        # Every backticked token on an "Only edit" line, not just the first.
        # `Only edit `a.py`, `b.py`` names TWO files; capturing only the first
        # backtick group made a legit edit to `b.py` a false scope FAIL. Also
        # split the comma-separated-in-one-backtick form (`a.py, b.py`).
        for m in re.finditer(r"[Oo]nly edit\b([^\n]*)", self.task_text):
            tail = m.group(1)
            # A scope line commonly reads "Only edit `x`; do not edit `verify.sh`,
            # `<fixture>` or `TASK.md`." -- and the REAL scaffold emits exactly
            # that. Everything after a "do not edit"/"don't edit" clause NAMES
            # FILES THE MODEL MUST NOT TOUCH; capturing their backticked tokens as
            # TARGETS mis-classified the fixture as a target, which emptied
            # fixture_py_files()/fixture_js_files() and silently made spec-gap and
            # draft-confirmed inert on the standard scaffold shape. Truncate the
            # tail at the first negated-edit clause so only the edit-ME tokens
            # count.
            tail = re.split(r"(?i)\bdo\s*n(?:['’]t|ot)\s+edit\b",
                            tail, maxsplit=1)[0]
            for group in re.findall(r"`([^`\n]+)`", tail):
                for tok in re.split(r"[,\s]+(?:and\s+)?", group):
                    tok = tok.strip("`,.; ")
                    if not tok or tok in seen:
                        continue
                    p = self.wt / tok
                    # An existing file always counts. For a CREATION task the
                    # target does not exist at baseline, so also accept a token
                    # that LOOKS like a source filename (a real code extension,
                    # no call-parens or operators) even when absent -- otherwise
                    # a create dispatch reaches "no target inferred" and the
                    # operator must pass --target by hand (papercut #5).
                    looks_like_src = (
                        bool(re.fullmatch(r"[\w./-]+\.[A-Za-z]\w*", tok))
                        and Path(tok).suffix in SRC_SUFFIXES)
                    if p.is_file() or looks_like_src:
                        seen.add(tok)
                        out.append(p)
        return out

    def check_target_parses(self):
        targets = self.target_files()
        if not targets:
            return self.unproven(
                "target-parses",
                "no target file inferred from the task and none given",
                fix="pass --target <file>, or give the task a scope line "
                    '("Only edit `x.py`")')
        bad, checked, absent = [], [], []
        for t in targets:
            is_py = t.suffix == ".py"
            is_ts = t.suffix in TS_SUFFIXES
            if not (is_py or is_ts):
                continue
            if is_ts and not TS_PARSE.is_file():
                # No sidecar to parse TS with -- skip THIS file rather than
                # silently passing it (the old code just `continue`d, so a TS
                # syntax error was never seen). Recorded via `absent`-style skip.
                continue
            if not t.is_file():
                # A CREATION task: the target does not exist yet. Not a defect,
                # and emphatically not a crash -- the gate used to die here with
                # FileNotFoundError, which reads as the gate being broken rather
                # than the task being a create.
                absent.append(t.name)
                continue
            checked.append(t)
            if is_py:
                try:
                    ast.parse(t.read_text(errors="replace"))
                except SyntaxError as e:
                    bad.append(f"{t.name}:{e.lineno}: {e.msg}")
            else:  # TS/JS -- delegate to the ts-parse sidecar (exits nonzero on
                   # a syntactic diagnostic, like ast.parse raising SyntaxError).
                p = subprocess.run(["node", str(TS_PARSE), str(t)],
                                   capture_output=True, text=True)
                if p.returncode != 0:
                    first = (p.stderr or p.stdout).strip().splitlines()
                    bad.append(first[0] if first else f"{t.name}: does not parse")
        if bad:
            return self.add("target-parses", FAIL,
                            "target does not parse", detail="; ".join(bad))
        if absent and not checked:
            return self.add(
                "target-parses", PASS,
                f"{', '.join(absent)} does not exist yet -- a creation task",
                detail="nothing to parse at baseline; the verify must assert "
                       "the file exists and parses after the model writes it")
        if not checked:
            return self.skipped(
                "target-parses",
                "no python target to parse ("
                + ", ".join(t.name for t in targets) + ")")
        return self.add("target-parses", PASS,
                        ", ".join(t.name for t in checked) + " parses")

    def check_verify_shape(self):
        vf = self.wt / "verify.sh"
        if not vf.exists():
            return self.unproven("verify-shape",
                                 "no verify.sh -- the verify's shape is unknown")
        src = vf.read_text(errors="replace")
        probs = []
        if re.search(r"^\s*set\s+-[a-z]*e", src, re.M):
            probs.append("`set -e` aborts on the first failing check, so the "
                         "model never sees the full FAIL list it is told to "
                         "work through (use `set -u` and a fails counter)")
        if "VERIFY_OK" not in src:
            probs.append("never prints VERIFY_OK -- the worker's success signal")
        if not re.search(r"exit\s+1", src):
            probs.append("has no non-zero exit path, so it cannot fail")
        if probs:
            return self.add("verify-shape", WARN, f"{len(probs)} shape issue(s)",
                            detail="; ".join(probs),
                            fix='end with: echo "--- $fails failed ---"; '
                                '[ "$fails" -eq 0 ] && echo VERIFY_OK || exit 1')
        return self.add("verify-shape", PASS, "counting idiom, VERIFY_OK, exit 1")

    # ---- dynamic checks ------------------------------------------------
    def check_baseline_fails(self):
        t0 = time.time()
        rc, out = self.sh(self.a.verify)
        self.verify_secs = time.time() - t0
        self.baseline_rc, self.baseline_out = rc, out
        if rc == 124:
            return self.add("baseline-fails", FAIL,
                            f"verify timed out after {VERIFY_TIMEOUT_S}s")
        if rc == 0 or "VERIFY_OK" in out:
            return self.add(
                "baseline-fails", FAIL,
                f"verify PASSES on the untouched baseline (exit {rc})",
                detail="It cannot tell a completed task from an untouched one. "
                       "A run that changes nothing would be certified done.",
                fix="make the verify assert the PROPERTY the fix introduces")
        return self.add("baseline-fails", PASS,
                        f"fails at baseline as it must (exit {rc}, "
                        f"{self.verify_secs:.1f}s)")

    def check_draft_confirmed(self):
        """Drafted cases must be read by a human before they gate a dispatch.

        The gate mechanically catches non-discriminating, unsatisfiable and
        benign-against-the-literals. It CANNOT catch a verify that
        discriminates, asserts every literal, and tests the wrong property --
        refimpl-satisfies is the decidable slice of relevance, not relevance.
        A model-drafted case that is merely irrelevant passes everything else
        here, so "a human looked" is a real property and gets a real check.
        """
        drafted, unreadable, seen = [], [], set()
        # Python fixtures carry `DRAFT_UNCONFIRMED = True`; JS/TS fixtures carry
        # `const DRAFT_UNCONFIRMED = true`. Scan BOTH families (and TASK.md) for
        # EITHER marker -- an unconfirmed draft in any of them means no human has
        # confirmed the cases. Scanning only .py fixtures let an unconfirmed
        # .mjs/.mts draft reach GO without the one irreducible relevance review.
        candidates = ([self.wt / self.a.task_file]
                      + self.fixture_py_files() + self.fixture_js_files())
        for f in candidates:
            try:
                rp = f.resolve()
            except Exception:
                rp = f
            if rp in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(rp)
            try:
                if not f.is_file():
                    continue
                text = f.read_text(errors="replace")
                if DRAFT_MARK in text or DRAFT_MARK_JS_RE.search(text):
                    drafted.append(f.name)
            except Exception as e:
                # Swallowing this made the check go COMPLETELY SILENT -- no
                # PASS, no FAIL, no UNPROVEN -- on an unreadable fixture, so an
                # unconfirmed model-drafted fixture became invisible to the
                # gate and reached GO. Third time this same invariant ("a human
                # read the cases") failed silently, after mark_drafted's no-op
                # re.sub. An absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.
                unreadable.append(f"{f.name} ({type(e).__name__})")
        if unreadable:
            return self.unproven(
                "draft-confirmed",
                f"could not read {len(unreadable)} file(s), so an unconfirmed "
                f"draft cannot be ruled out: {'; '.join(unreadable)}",
                fix="make the file readable and re-run; until then nobody has "
                    "established that a human reviewed these cases")
        if not drafted:
            return
        self.add(
            "draft-confirmed", FAIL,
            f"{len(drafted)} file(s) hold UNCONFIRMED model-drafted cases",
            detail=", ".join(drafted),
            fix="read every case and ask 'could this pass on broken work?', "
                "then: ollama-dispatch-draft <worktree> --confirm. The gate "
                "proved these DISCRIMINATE; only you can say they are "
                "RELEVANT.")

    def check_scaffold_authored(self):
        """A generated scaffold is not a verify.

        `ollama-dispatch-scaffold` emits a fixture with an EMPTY case list and a
        verify that prints SCAFFOLD_INCOMPLETE, deliberately: a generator can
        emit a verify that DISCRIMINATES but cannot decide whether it is
        RELEVANT, and that judgement is the one step nothing mechanises. Without
        this check the resulting NO-GO lands on verify-quality -- true, but a
        downstream symptom that sends you to rewrite the wrong thing.
        """
        if SCAFFOLD_MARK not in (self.baseline_out or ""):
            return
        self.add(
            "scaffold-authored", FAIL,
            "the generated scaffold has no adversarial cases yet",
            detail=(self.baseline_out or "").strip()[-300:],
            fix="author >= 3 adversarial cases in the fixture: the boundary "
                "and both sides of it, the degenerate inputs that must not "
                "raise, at least one a plausible WRONG fix would fail, and the "
                "regression half. Then re-run this gate.")

    def check_env_parity(self):
        """Did the baseline run fail for the RIGHT reason?

        Behavioural, not structural: a verify that dies on ImportError fails on
        every iteration for a reason that has nothing to do with the task, and
        the model has no way to make it stop.
        """
        if self.baseline_rc is None:
            return self.unproven("env-parity", "the baseline run never happened")
        out = self.baseline_out
        # (pattern, label, tailored one-line fix). A JS/TS baseline dies on the
        # environment two ways the generic venv fix does not address: a missing
        # node_modules package (often a SUB-PACKAGE's own deps the root symlink
        # misses) and an IMPORT-TIME `throw` on an unset required env var. Both
        # are now auto-handled by the scaffold's bootstrap; surface the specific
        # module/var + fix here as a backstop when one still slips through.
        _venv_fix = ("bootstrap a venv in verify.sh and run every check through "
                     "it: PY=.venv/bin/python; [ -x \"$PY\" ] || { python3 -m "
                     "venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -q -r "
                     "requirements.txt; }")
        sigs = [
            (r"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named ['\"]?([\w.]+)",
             "missing python module", _venv_fix),
            (r"Cannot find (?:package|module) ['\"]([^'\"]+)['\"]",
             "missing node module",
             "the target imports a package the worktree's node_modules lacks -- "
             "if it lives under a sub-package (its own package.json + "
             "node_modules), symlink that sub-package's node_modules from the "
             "source in verify.sh (re-scaffold to auto-detect); else `npm ci`"),
            (r"([A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{2,})\s+env(?:ironment)?[ _]?var(?:iable)?\s+is\s+"
             r"required", "import-time env var required",
             "the target throws at import when this env var is unset -- export a "
             "dummy in verify.sh's bootstrap AND name it in TASK.md's Test "
             "environment section (re-scaffold to auto-detect both)"),
            (r"ImportError: ([^\n]{0,80})", "import error", _venv_fix),
            (r"([\w./-]+): command not found", "missing command", _venv_fix),
            (r"No such file or directory: ['\"]([^'\"]+)", "missing file",
             _venv_fix),
            (r"externally-managed-environment", "PEP-668: system pip is blocked",
             _venv_fix),
        ]
        hits, chosen_fix = [], None
        for pat, label, fix in sigs:
            m = re.search(pat, out)
            if m:
                hits.append(f"{label}: {m.group(1) if m.groups() else m.group(0)}")
                if chosen_fix is None:
                    chosen_fix = fix
        if hits:
            return self.add(
                "env-parity", FAIL,
                "the baseline run died on the ENVIRONMENT, not on the task",
                detail="; ".join(hits[:3]),
                fix=chosen_fix or _venv_fix)
        has_req = (self.wt / "requirements.txt").exists()
        vf = self.wt / "verify.sh"
        bootstraps = vf.exists() and "venv" in vf.read_text(errors="replace")
        if has_req and not bootstraps:
            return self.add("env-parity", WARN,
                            "repo has requirements.txt but verify.sh never "
                            "bootstraps a venv",
                            detail="it happens to work now; it will break the "
                                   "first time the target imports a dep")
        return self.add("env-parity", PASS,
                        "baseline failed on the task, not the environment")

    def check_verify_speed(self):
        if self.verify_secs is None:
            return self.unproven("verify-speed", "the baseline run never happened")
        if self.verify_secs > SLOW_VERIFY_S:
            return self.add("verify-speed", WARN,
                            f"verify takes {self.verify_secs:.0f}s",
                            detail=f"it runs after every edit; over "
                                   f"{SLOW_VERIFY_S}s the loop crawls and "
                                   f"iterations get spent waiting")
        return self.add("verify-speed", PASS, f"{self.verify_secs:.1f}s per "
                                              f"iteration")

    def apply_refimpl(self):
        if self.a.refimpl:
            p = Path(self.a.refimpl).expanduser().resolve()
            if not p.exists():
                return False, f"--refimpl {p} does not exist"
            rc, out = self.sh(f"git apply --verbose {json.dumps(str(p))}",
                              timeout=120)
            if rc != 0:
                rc2, out2 = self.sh(f"patch -p1 -i {json.dumps(str(p))}",
                                    timeout=120)
                if rc2 != 0:
                    return False, f"could not apply patch: {out.strip()[-300:]}"
            return True, "patch applied"
        rc, out = self.sh(self.a.refimpl_cmd, timeout=600)
        if rc != 0:
            return False, f"--refimpl-cmd exited {rc}: {out.strip()[-300:]}"
        return True, "refimpl command ran"

    def revert_refimpl(self):
        """Restore tracked files; delete only files the refimpl itself added.

        NOT `git clean -fdx` -- that would take TASK.md, verify.sh, the fixture
        and .venv with it. The untracked set is diffed against the pre-refimpl
        snapshot so exactly the refimpl's own new files go and nothing else does.
        """
        self.sh("git checkout -- .", timeout=120)
        rc, out, _ = self.git("status", "--porcelain")
        after = {l[3:].strip() for l in out.splitlines() if l[:2] == "??"}
        # Reference is the untracked set as it stood JUST BEFORE the refimpl was
        # applied (LOW11) -- not the pre-baseline set, which predates any .venv
        # or __pycache__ the baseline verify created. Fall back to the
        # pre-baseline set only if the pre-refimpl snapshot is unavailable.
        ref = getattr(self, "_pre_refimpl_untracked", None)
        if ref is None:
            ref = getattr(self, "untracked_before", set())
        for rel in sorted(after - ref):
            p = self.wt / rel
            try:
                if p.is_dir():
                    shutil.rmtree(p)
                elif p.exists():
                    p.unlink()
            except Exception:
                pass

    def must_contain_literals(self):
        """The `## Must contain` bullets -- what the spec obliges to appear."""
        m = re.search(r"##+\s*Must contain[^\n]*\n(.*?)(?=\n##\s|\Z)",
                      self.task_text, re.S | re.I)
        if not m:
            return []
        return [b.group(1) for b in re.finditer(r"`([^`\n]{4,200})`", m.group(1))]

    @staticmethod
    def _literal_present(lit, blob):
        """Is a Must-contain literal satisfied by the source blob?

        Exact substring is the rule. The one loosening: a literal that is a
        WHOLE quoted string -- `"processing"` in the Must-contain list -- is
        satisfied by the same string under the OTHER quote style in the source
        (`status = 'processing'`). Python treats "x" and 'x' as identical, so a
        double-quoted spec literal FAILing against single-quoted source was a
        false NO-GO (papercut #5). Only fully-quoted literals are loosened, so
        a behavioural code token that merely contains a quote is untouched.
        """
        if lit in blob:
            return True
        if len(lit) >= 2 and lit[0] in "\"'" and lit[-1] == lit[0]:
            inner = lit[1:-1]
            if lit[0] not in inner:  # no embedded same-quote to complicate
                alt = "'" if lit[0] == '"' else '"'
                if alt + inner + alt in blob:
                    return True
        return False

    def refimpl_scope_text(self):
        """The files the CHANGE is allowed to live in -- not the whole repo.

        This check used to concatenate every tracked file and ask whether the
        literal appeared anywhere. In the purpose-built fixtures it did not, so
        it looked sound. In a real repo it is inert: a "Must contain" literal
        names a symbol you are integrating with, and that symbol is already
        somewhere in the tree. Caught on the first real worktree --
        machine-config, where `eval_arms_pending` already lived in
        bin/handoff-emit.py and a deliberately benign verify was rendered GO.
        An inert check is worse than no check: it looks like a check that passed.

        Scope is the files the refimpl TOUCHED, plus the declared target. Not
        the diff's added lines: a "Must contain" literal is as often one that
        must be PRESERVED (a signature the fix must not change) as one that must
        be added, and diff-scoping fails those. Not pre-existing untracked files
        either -- the verify fixture names the very keys the spec lists, so
        counting it re-inerts the check by a second route.
        """
        rel_paths = set()
        rc, names, _ = self.git("diff", "--name-only")
        if rc == 0:
            rel_paths |= {l.strip() for l in names.splitlines() if l.strip()}
        before = getattr(self, "untracked_before", set())
        rc, untracked, _ = self.git("ls-files", "--others", "--exclude-standard")
        if rc == 0:
            rel_paths |= {l.strip() for l in untracked.splitlines()
                          if l.strip() and l.strip() not in before}
        rel_paths -= {STATE_FILE, *self.scaffold_rel_paths()}
        paths = {self.wt / r for r in rel_paths}
        paths |= {t for t in self.target_files() if t.is_file()}
        blob = []
        for f in sorted(paths):
            try:
                if f.is_file() and f.stat().st_size < 4_000_000:
                    blob.append(f.read_text(errors="replace"))
            except Exception:
                pass
        return "\n".join(blob)

    def check_refimpl(self):
        """Both-ways half 2, plus the relevance probe.

        Two distinct facts come out of one run:
          - the task is SATISFIABLE as specified (verify can be made to pass);
          - the verify actually ENFORCES the spec. If a reference impl turns it
            green while a "Must contain" literal is absent from the tree, then a
            model can do the same -- that is a benign verify, caught before it
            passes broken work rather than after.
        """
        if self.a.no_refimpl:
            self.unproven(
                "refimpl-passes",
                "WAIVED (--no-refimpl): nobody has shown this task is "
                "satisfiable or that a correct fix goes green",
                fix="an unsatisfiable task burns every iteration the model has")
            self.unproven("refimpl-satisfies",
                          "WAIVED: nothing proved the verify ENFORCES the spec, "
                          "so a benign verify would pass broken work")
            self.unproven("verify-relevance",
                          "WAIVED with the refimpl: no reference impl, nothing "
                          "to mutate, relevance unmeasured")
            self.skipped("refimpl-reverted", "no refimpl was applied")
            return
        # Snapshot the tree state IMMEDIATELY before applying the reference
        # impl, so revert/reverted accounting is relative to here -- not to the
        # pre-baseline snapshot. Two bugs this closes:
        #   LOW11  untracked_before was captured before check_baseline_fails ran
        #          the verify, which may have created .venv/__pycache__/fixture
        #          outputs. Diffing revert against it deleted THOSE too. The
        #          pre-refimpl set contains them, so only the refimpl's own new
        #          files are removed.
        #   LOW9   a --refimpl-cmd that COMMITS leaves the tree clean with HEAD
        #          advanced, so `git checkout -- .` cannot revert it and
        #          refimpl-reverted would PASS while the answer sits in HEAD.
        self._pre_refimpl_head = self.git("rev-parse", "HEAD")[1].strip()
        rc, out, _ = self.git("status", "--porcelain")
        self._pre_refimpl_untracked = {
            l[3:].strip() for l in out.splitlines() if l[:2] == "??"}
        ok, why = self.apply_refimpl()
        if not ok:
            self.add("refimpl-passes", FAIL, "could not apply the reference impl",
                     detail=why)
            self.unproven("refimpl-satisfies",
                          "the refimpl never applied, so the verify's grip on "
                          "the spec was never tested")
            self.unproven("verify-relevance",
                          "the refimpl never applied, so nothing was mutated")
            self.revert_refimpl()
            self.check_reverted()
            return
        rc, out = self.sh(self.a.verify)
        green = (rc == 0 and "VERIFY_OK" in out)
        if green:
            self.add("refimpl-passes", PASS,
                     "reference impl makes the verify print VERIFY_OK")
        else:
            self.add("refimpl-passes", FAIL,
                     f"reference impl does NOT satisfy the verify (exit {rc})",
                     detail=(out.strip()[-400:] or "no output"),
                     fix="either the task is unsatisfiable as written or the "
                         "verify asserts something the spec never asked for -- "
                         "the model would fail this too, indefinitely")
        lits = self.must_contain_literals()
        if not lits:
            self.unproven(
                "refimpl-satisfies",
                'the task has no "Must contain" literals, so there is nothing '
                'mechanical to hold the verify against',
                fix="add a `## Must contain` list of literal tokens to the task")
        else:
            blob = self.refimpl_scope_text()
            absent = [l for l in lits if not self._literal_present(l, blob)]
            if absent and green:
                self.add(
                    "refimpl-satisfies", FAIL,
                    f"the verify goes GREEN while {len(absent)} required "
                    f"literal(s) are absent from the changed/target file(s)",
                    detail="; ".join(repr(l) for l in absent[:4]),
                    fix="the verify does not enforce the spec -- a model can "
                        "pass it without doing what was asked. Assert the "
                        "property behaviourally (a whole literal cannot match "
                        "its own negation).")
            elif absent:
                self.add("refimpl-satisfies", WARN,
                         f"{len(absent)} required literal(s) absent from the "
                         f"reference impl's change",
                         detail="; ".join(repr(l) for l in absent[:4])
                                + " -- the refimpl and the spec disagree")
            else:
                self.add("refimpl-satisfies", PASS,
                         f"reference impl contains all {len(lits)} required "
                         f"literal(s)")
        if green:
            self.check_verify_relevance()
        else:
            self.unproven("verify-relevance",
                          "the reference impl is not green, so there is nothing "
                          "to mutate against")
        self.revert_refimpl()
        self.check_reverted()

    def check_verify_relevance(self):
        """Delegated to verify-relevance.py, run while the refimpl is applied.

        This is the check that closes the gap draft-confirmed documents: the
        gate could prove a verify DISCRIMINATES but not that it is RELEVANT,
        so a human read of the cases was mandatory. Mutation relevance is the
        machine-checkable slice of that read -- it cannot say the cases are
        the RIGHT ones, but it can say whether the verify notices when the fix
        it just accepted is broken in the obvious ways. Survivors are named so
        the human read, where it still happens, starts from the holes.

        Not merely a withheld auto-approve: a LOW-relevance verify is a hard NO-GO that blocks the dispatch entirely -- a broken fix must not survive its own verify, so signoff.py reads this evidence from the ledger and refuses to proceed.
        """
        if getattr(self.a, "no_relevance", False):
            return self.unproven(
                "verify-relevance",
                "WAIVED (--no-relevance): nothing measured whether the verify "
                "tests the property or a proxy for it",
                fix="a low-relevance verify cannot auto-approve; a waived one "
                    "cannot either")
        vr = BIN / "verify-relevance.py"
        if not vr.exists():
            return self.unproven(
                "verify-relevance", f"{vr} not found -- relevance is UNJUDGED",
                fix="install verify-relevance.py next to this gate")
        try:
            spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("verify_relevance", vr)
            mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
            spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
        except Exception as e:
            return self.unproven("verify-relevance",
                                 f"verify-relevance.py could not load: {e}")
        rc, diff_text, _ = self.git("diff", "-U0")
        if rc != 0 or not diff_text.strip():
            return self.unproven("verify-relevance",
                                 "the reference impl produced no tracked diff to "
                                 "mutate (added files only?)")
        try:
            rec = mod.measure_applied(
                self.wt, self.a.verify, diff_text,
                literals=self.must_contain_literals(),
                max_mutants=getattr(self.a, "relevance_max_mutants", 40),
                budget_s=getattr(self.a, "relevance_budget_s", 600),
                skip_green_check=True)
        except Exception as e:
            return self.unproven("verify-relevance",
                                 f"measurement crashed: {type(e).__name__}: {e}")
        # Keep the record small enough to live in the state file and ledger:
        # summary + survivors, not every mutant's full source.
        slim = {k: v for k, v in rec.items() if k not in ("mutants",)}
        slim["survivors"] = [{k: v for k, v in m.items() if k != "source"}
                             for m in rec.get("survivors", [])]
        slim["verify_sha256"] = self.scaffold_digests().get("verify.sh")
        self._state["verify_relevance"] = slim
        v = rec["verdict"]
        surv = "; ".join(f"{m['file']}:{m['line']} {m['mutation']}"
                         for m in rec.get("survivors", [])[:4])
        unex = rec.get("unexercised_sites") or []
        if v == "relevant":
            return self.add("verify-relevance", PASS,
                            f"verify killed {rec['killed']}/{rec['evidence_mutants']} "
                            f"property-breaking mutants (score {rec['score']}, "
                            f"{rec['seconds']}s)",
                            detail=("survivors: " + surv) if surv else "")
        if v == "low":
            return self.add(
                "verify-relevance", FAIL,
                f"LOW relevance: {rec['survived']}/{rec['evidence_mutants']} "
                f"property-breaking mutants survived (score {rec['score']})",
                detail=(("never exercised: " + ", ".join(unex) + ". ") if unex else "")
                       + "survivors: " + surv,
                fix="the verify is testing a proxy or a benign slice of the "
                    "property. Add the case each survivor would fail. This "
                    "dispatch is BLOCKED (NO-GO) until relevance is proven.")
        return self.unproven("verify-relevance", rec.get("reason") or
                             "relevance could not be measured",
                             fix="a dispatch whose relevance is unmeasured "
                                 "cannot auto-approve")

    def check_reverted(self):
        rc, out, _ = self.git("status", "--porcelain")
        left = [l for l in out.splitlines() if l[:2] != "??" and l.strip()]
        ref = getattr(self, "_pre_refimpl_untracked", None)
        if ref is None:
            ref = getattr(self, "untracked_before", set())
        stray = ({l[3:].strip() for l in out.splitlines() if l[:2] == "??"} - ref)
        # LOW9: a refimpl that COMMITTED its change leaves a clean working tree
        # with HEAD advanced -- `git checkout -- .` cannot undo a commit, so
        # without this the model would begin from the answer sitting in HEAD.
        pre = getattr(self, "_pre_refimpl_head", None)
        now_head = self.git("rev-parse", "HEAD")[1].strip() if pre else None
        head_moved = bool(pre and now_head and now_head != pre)
        if left or stray or head_moved:
            detail = "; ".join(left[:4] + [f"?? {s}" for s in sorted(stray)[:4]])
            if head_moved:
                extra = f"HEAD advanced {pre[:12]} -> {now_head[:12]} (refimpl COMMITTED)"
                detail = (detail + "; " + extra) if detail else extra
            return self.add(
                "refimpl-reverted", FAIL,
                "the tree is NOT back at baseline after the reference impl"
                + (" -- the refimpl COMMITTED its change, so the answer is in "
                   "HEAD and `git checkout` cannot revert it" if head_moved else ""),
                detail=detail,
                fix=("reset HEAD to the gate baseline "
                     f"(git -C {self.wt} reset --hard {pre[:12]}) and make the "
                     "refimpl NOT commit" if head_moved else
                     "the model would start from the answer -- restore with "
                     f"git -C {self.wt} checkout -- ."))
        return self.add("refimpl-reverted", PASS,
                        "tree restored to baseline; the model starts clean")

    def check_verify_quality(self):
        """Delegated. This tool does not judge verify quality itself."""
        vq = BIN / "verify-quality.py"
        if not vq.exists():
            return self.unproven(
                "verify-quality",
                f"{vq} not found -- verify soundness is UNJUDGED",
                fix="this tool delegates gameability judgement rather than "
                    "carrying a second copy; without it, nothing checked "
                    "whether the verify can tell correct from inverted")
        cmd = [sys.executable, str(vq), "--verify", self.a.verify,
               "--cwd", str(self.wt), "--task-file",
               str(self.wt / self.a.task_file), "--no-baseline"]
        # --no-baseline: this tool already ran the baseline itself (check
        # baseline-fails). Running it twice doubles the slowest step for a fact
        # already established.
        try:
            p = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=600)
        except Exception as e:
            return self.unproven("verify-quality",
                                 f"verify-quality.py could not run: {e}")
        out = (p.stdout + p.stderr).strip()
        if p.returncode == 0:
            return self.add("verify-quality", PASS,
                            "verify-quality.py: sound",
                            detail=out[-300:])
        if p.returncode == 2:
            return self.unproven("verify-quality",
                                 f"verify-quality.py errored (exit 2), so it "
                                 f"judged nothing: {out[-200:]}")
        return self.add("verify-quality", FAIL,
                        "verify-quality.py: verify is NOT discriminating",
                        detail=out[-600:])

    # ---- driver --------------------------------------------------------
    def scaffold_rel_paths(self):
        """Relative paths of every file the MODEL MUST NOT TOUCH.

        NOTE `--verify` is a COMMAND ("bash verify.sh"), not a filename.
        Treating it as a path silently hashed nothing and left the verify --
        the one file whose tampering matters most -- unprotected. Pull the .sh
        tokens out of the command instead.
        """
        rels, seen = [], set()
        # The DECLARED TARGET is the one file the model is supposed to edit.
        # followed_py_files() finds it anyway, because verify.sh legitimately
        # names it (`open('target.py')`), and protecting it as scaffold flags
        # the intended fix as tampering. Scaffold means untouchable; the target
        # is the opposite of that.
        targets = set()
        for f in self.target_files():
            try:
                targets.add(str(f.relative_to(self.wt)))
            except Exception:
                pass

        def take(rel):
            rel = _norm_rel(rel)
            if (rel and rel not in seen and rel not in targets
                    and (self.wt / rel).is_file()):
                seen.add(rel)
                rels.append(rel)

        take(self.a.task_file)
        for m in re.finditer(r"([\w./-]+\.sh)\b", self.a.verify or ""):
            take(m.group(1))
        take("verify.sh")
        for f in self.followed_py_files():
            try:
                take(str(f.relative_to(self.wt)))
            except Exception:
                pass
        return rels

    def scaffold_digests(self):
        """sha256 of every file the MODEL MUST NOT TOUCH.

        The task, the verify, and the fixtures the verify runs. A model that
        edits its own verify or fixture passes trivially, and the green result
        is indistinguishable from a real one unless you recorded what they
        looked like before.
        """
        out, failed = {}, []
        for rel in self.scaffold_rel_paths():
            try:
                out[rel] = hashlib.sha256(
                    (self.wt / rel).read_bytes()).hexdigest()
            except Exception as e:
                # A file omitted from the contract cannot be checked for
                # tampering by --review later, and silently omitting it makes
                # scaffold-untouched report PASS over a file it never hashed.
                failed.append(f"{rel} ({type(e).__name__})")
        if failed:
            out["__unhashable__"] = failed
        return out

    def _audit_untracked(self, prev, new_untracked):
        """MED4: files that appeared since the gate baseline.

        The scope audit used to skip ALL untracked files ("not the model's
        doing") -- but a root-level `*.py`/`*.mjs` the model dropped can SHADOW a
        stdlib or local import, because the fixture runs with the worktree as
        sys.path[0] (it precedes stdlib). A `hashlib.py`/`subprocess.py` at the
        worktree root then makes the import resolve to the model's file, and both
        the scaffold and scope audits pass over the tampering. Those are a FAIL;
        other new untracked files are a WARN.
        """
        before = set(prev.get("untracked_before") or [])
        have_baseline = "untracked_before" in prev

        def noise(p):
            first = p.split("/", 1)[0]
            return first.startswith(".") or first in ("__pycache__", "node_modules")

        added = [p for p in new_untracked if p not in before and not noise(p)]
        shadow = [p for p in added
                  if "/" not in p and Path(p).suffix in (".py", ".mjs")]
        if shadow and have_baseline:
            return self.add(
                "scope-untracked", FAIL,
                f"{len(shadow)} new root-level module(s) the gate never saw -- "
                "these SHADOW a stdlib/local import (the fixture runs with the "
                "worktree as sys.path[0])",
                detail=", ".join(shadow),
                fix="delete them; a dropped hashlib.py/subprocess.py at the "
                    "worktree root makes the import resolve to the model's file "
                    "and the scaffold/scope audits pass over tampering")
        if shadow:
            return self.add(
                "scope-untracked", WARN,
                f"{len(shadow)} new root-level module(s) present, but the gate "
                "record predates untracked-set recording so they cannot be "
                "confirmed new",
                detail=", ".join(shadow))
        if have_baseline and added:
            return self.add(
                "scope-untracked", WARN,
                f"{len(added)} untracked file(s) not present at the gate baseline",
                detail=", ".join(sorted(added)[:8]))

    def check_scope_respected(self, prev, committed_files=None):
        """Did the model change ONLY what the task declared?

        A green verify says nothing about files the verify does not touch. On
        the Sonarr reverse-pack dispatch a model DELETED a tracked test file
        unrelated to the task and the verify stayed green the whole time,
        because the deleted file was not part of that fix's verify. A less
        careful merge -- or an auto-approve -- would have shipped a silent
        deletion of a tracked test.

        Deletions are named separately from edits: an out-of-scope edit shows up
        in a diff review, a deletion is an absence and reviewers do not notice
        absences.
        """
        scope = set(prev.get("declared_scope") or [])
        rc, out, _ = self.git("status", "--porcelain")
        if rc != 0:
            return self.unproven("scope-respected", "git status failed")
        deleted, changed, new_untracked = [], [], []
        for line in out.splitlines():
            if not line.strip():
                continue
            code, path = line[:2], line[3:].strip()
            if code == "??":
                if path != STATE_FILE:
                    new_untracked.append(path)
                continue
            if " -> " in path:    # a rename reports both halves
                path = path.split(" -> ", 1)[1]
            if path in scope:
                continue
            # .preflight-state.json is NO LONGER exempt: a TRACKED-and-MODIFIED
            # state file is a rewrite of the very contract this audit trusts, so
            # it must FAIL, not pass silently (HIGH2).
            (deleted if "D" in code else changed).append(f"{code.strip()} {path}")
        # committed base..HEAD changes (MED7) -- invisible to `git status`.
        for path in (committed_files or []):
            if path in scope:
                continue
            changed.append(f"commit {path}")
        # untracked additions the gate never saw (MED4).
        self._audit_untracked(prev, new_untracked)
        if not scope:
            return self.unproven(
                "scope-respected",
                "the gate run recorded no declared scope, so out-of-scope "
                "changes cannot be distinguished from intended ones",
                fix='give the task a scope line ("Only edit `x.py`") or pass '
                    "--target, then re-run the gate before dispatching")
        if deleted or changed:
            return self.add(
                "scope-respected", FAIL,
                f"{len(deleted) + len(changed)} tracked file(s) changed OUTSIDE "
                f"the declared scope"
                + (f" -- {len(deleted)} DELETED" if deleted else ""),
                detail="; ".join(deleted + changed),
                fix="a green verify does not cover files the verify never "
                    "touches. Restore them (`git checkout -- <path>`) and "
                    "review why the model went outside "
                    + ", ".join(sorted(scope)))
        return self.add("scope-respected", PASS,
                        f"only the {len(scope)} declared file(s) changed")

    def check_scaffold_untouched(self, prev):
        """The model must not have edited the task, the verify, or a fixture."""
        want = prev.get("scaffold_sha256") or {}
        if not want:
            return self.unproven(
                "scaffold-untouched",
                "the gate run recorded no scaffold digests, so verify/fixture "
                "tampering cannot be ruled out",
                fix="re-run the gate before dispatching so it records them")
        unhashable = want.pop("__unhashable__", None)
        if unhashable:
            self.add("scaffold-untouched", WARN,
                     f"{len(unhashable)} scaffold file(s) could not be hashed "
                     "at gate time, so tampering in them is UNCHECKED",
                     detail="; ".join(unhashable))
        bad = []
        for rel, sha in want.items():
            f = self.wt / rel
            try:
                now = hashlib.sha256(f.read_bytes()).hexdigest() if f.is_file() else None
            except Exception:
                now = None
            if now is None:
                bad.append(f"{rel} (GONE)")
            elif now != sha:
                bad.append(rel)
        if bad:
            return self.add(
                "scaffold-untouched", FAIL,
                f"{len(bad)} scaffold file(s) changed during the run",
                detail="; ".join(bad),
                fix="the model edited its own task/verify/fixture -- the green "
                    "verify proves nothing. Restore and re-dispatch with the "
                    "scope line naming these files explicitly.")
        return self.add("scaffold-untouched", PASS,
                        f"all {len(want)} scaffold file(s) byte-identical: "
                        + ", ".join(sorted(want)))

    def run_review(self):
        """POST-RUN scope audit. READ-ONLY -- never mutates the tree.

        The pre-dispatch half of this tool applies a refimpl and reverts it.
        That is exactly what must NOT happen here: this runs on a finished job's
        tree, and the fix is the thing being reviewed.
        """
        if self.check_worktree() == FAIL:
            return self.report(review=True)
        sf = self.wt / STATE_FILE
        lp = self.ledger_path_for(self.wt)
        # Resolve the scope contract, PREFERRING the ledger. The ledger lives
        # OUTSIDE the worktree, so unlike the in-tree .preflight-state.json it
        # is not a file the finished job could have rewritten. Both are held to
        # the declared_scope-present rule (load_state require_scope) so a
        # non-empty but contract-less in-tree file cannot SHADOW a real contract
        # -- the exact gap that let a model widen declared_scope and re-hash its
        # edited fixture and pass review (HIGH2). This also back-ports the
        # integrator's resolve_contract for the `--repo` auto-worktree flow,
        # which carries no in-tree state but does record to the ledger.
        in_tree = self.load_state(sf, require_scope=True) if sf.exists() else {}
        ledger = self.load_state(lp, require_scope=True) if lp.exists() else {}

        CONTRACT_KEYS = ("declared_scope", "scaffold_sha256", "baseline_commit")
        prev, contract_source, mismatch = {}, None, False
        if in_tree and ledger:
            # BOTH exist: they MUST agree, or the in-tree copy has been tampered
            # with. Audit against the authoritative ledger regardless.
            mism = [k for k in CONTRACT_KEYS if in_tree.get(k) != ledger.get(k)]
            prev, contract_source = ledger, f"ledger {lp.name}"
            if mism:
                mismatch = True
                self.add(
                    "contract-source", FAIL,
                    "in-tree .preflight-state.json DISAGREES with the external "
                    "ledger on the scope contract (contract-mismatch)",
                    detail="mismatched field(s): " + ", ".join(mism)
                           + " -- the in-tree copy may have been rewritten to "
                             "widen scope or re-hash an edited fixture; auditing "
                             "against the authoritative ledger",
                    fix="restore or delete the in-tree state and re-review; the "
                        "ledger outside the tree is the contract of record")
        elif ledger:
            prev, contract_source = ledger, f"ledger {lp.name}"
        elif in_tree:
            prev, contract_source = in_tree, "in-tree state"
        if not prev:
            self.unproven("scope-respected",
                          f"no {STATE_FILE} and no ledger entry (with a declared "
                          "scope) -- this tree was never gated, so there is no "
                          "recorded scope to hold it against")
            self.unproven("scaffold-untouched", "no gate run to compare against")
            return self.report(review=True)
        if not mismatch:
            self.add("contract-source", PASS,
                     f"scope contract resolved from {contract_source}")
        base = prev.get("baseline_commit")
        now = self.git("rev-parse", "HEAD")[1].strip()
        committed = []
        if base and now != base:
            # HEAD moved. The scope audit below diffs the WORKING TREE against
            # HEAD, so a model that COMMITTED an out-of-scope edit is invisible
            # to it. Fold the committed range into the audit too (MED7).
            self.add("same-baseline", WARN,
                     "HEAD moved since the gate run",
                     detail=f"gated at {base[:12]}, now {now[:12]}; also auditing "
                            f"the committed range {base[:12]}..{now[:12]}")
            rc, names, _ = self.git("diff", "--name-only", f"{base}..{now}")
            if rc == 0:
                committed = [l.strip() for l in names.splitlines() if l.strip()]
        self.check_scope_respected(prev, committed_files=committed)
        self.check_scaffold_untouched(prev)
        return self.report(review=True)

    def run(self):
        if self.check_worktree() == FAIL:
            return self.report()
        # Before ANYTHING that touches the tree: this gate applies a refimpl,
        # so running it inside another job's worktree can hand that job the
        # answer mid-run.
        if self.check_cwd_exclusive() == FAIL:
            for cid in ("baseline-clean", "baseline-fails", "env-parity",
                        "verify-speed", "verify-quality", "refimpl-passes",
                        "refimpl-satisfies", "verify-relevance",
                        "refimpl-reverted"):
                self.unproven(cid, "not run: another active job owns this "
                                   "tree, and this gate mutates it")
            return self.report()
        # Toolchain floors (gap #3) do not touch the tree and are worth naming
        # even on a dirty baseline, so they run before baseline-clean.
        self.check_toolchain()
        base_ok = self.check_baseline_clean()
        # Fold `--seal-baseline` into the GO decision (gap #2) -- needs the
        # untracked set baseline-clean just populated.
        self.check_harness_sealed()
        self.check_task_shape()
        self.check_task_unchanged()
        self.check_required_literals()
        self.check_heredoc_escaping()
        self.check_spec_gap()
        self.check_target_parses()
        self.check_verify_shape()

        if base_ok == FAIL:
            for cid in ("baseline-fails", "env-parity", "verify-speed",
                        "verify-quality", "refimpl-passes",
                        "refimpl-satisfies", "verify-relevance",
                        "refimpl-reverted"):
                self.unproven(cid, "not run: the tree is not at baseline, so a "
                                   "run here would prove nothing")
            return self.report()

        self.check_baseline_fails()
        self.check_draft_confirmed()
        self.check_scaffold_authored()
        self.check_env_parity()
        self.check_verify_speed()
        self.check_verify_quality()
        self.check_refimpl()
        return self.report()

    @staticmethod
    def ledger_path_for(worktree) -> Path:
        """One ledger entry per worktree path. The path is the join key the
        gate record carries (job_cwd), so signoff.py can find it without
        reading anything from inside the tree."""
        key = hashlib.sha256(str(Path(worktree).resolve()).encode()).hexdigest()[:16]
        return PREFLIGHT_LEDGER / f"{key}.json"

    def write_ledger(self, st: dict):
        """Copy the decision-bearing evidence OUTSIDE the worktree.

        Best-effort and silent on failure: the ledger is an extra copy for the
        autonomous consumer, and a gate that cannot write it must still print
        its verdict. A missing ledger entry reads downstream as "relevance
        never measured", which blocks auto-approve -- the safe direction.
        """
        try:
            PREFLIGHT_LEDGER.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            entry = {
                "worktree": str(self.wt),
                "at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()),
                "verdict": st.get("verdict"),
                "baseline_commit": st.get("baseline_commit"),
                "declared_scope": st.get("declared_scope"),
                "scaffold_sha256": st.get("scaffold_sha256"),
                "untracked_before": st.get("untracked_before"),
                "verify_failed_at_baseline": st.get("verify_failed_at_baseline"),
                "verify_relevance": st.get("verify_relevance"),
                "unproven": st.get("unproven"),
                "blockers": st.get("blockers"),
            }
            # Atomic (LOW10): a plain write_text lets a concurrent reader
            # (signoff.py) catch a half-written file, parse it as {}, and read
            # "relevance never measured" -> "never gated". Write a sibling temp
            # and os.replace it into place, which is atomic on the same fs.
            target = self.ledger_path_for(self.wt)
            tmp = target.with_name(target.name + f".tmp.{os.getpid()}")
            tmp.write_text(json.dumps(entry, indent=1))
            os.replace(tmp, target)
        except Exception:
            pass

    def report(self, review=False):
        blockers = [r for r in self.results if r.status == FAIL]
        warns = [r for r in self.results if r.status == WARN]
        unproven = [r for r in self.results if r.status == UNPROVEN]
        verdict = "NO-GO" if blockers else "GO"

        sf = self.wt / STATE_FILE
        try:
            st = getattr(self, "_state", {})
            st["verdict"] = verdict
            st["blockers"] = [r.id for r in blockers]
            st["unproven"] = [r.id for r in unproven]
            # The authoritative both-ways reading, taken here BEFORE any model
            # edit. The Research session's auto-approve needs exactly this fact
            # and found it absent from all 85 gate records; publishing it from
            # the one place that actually measures it beats re-deriving it
            # downstream from a tree that has since been edited.
            st["verify_failed_at_baseline"] = (
                None if self.baseline_rc is None else self.baseline_rc != 0)
            st["verify_seconds"] = self.verify_secs
            # THE SCOPE CONTRACT (baseline_commit / declared_scope /
            # scaffold_sha256 / untracked_before). --review later holds the
            # finished tree against exactly this. It is only trustworthy when
            # the tree was AT BASELINE when we hashed it: on a run where
            # baseline-clean FAILED (a dirty or tampered tree, or a run that
            # never reached baseline-clean), hashing the CURRENT files would pin
            # the tampered content as the contract, and a later --review would
            # then report the tampered scaffold "untouched" (HIGH1). So record
            # the contract ONLY on a clean baseline; otherwise carry the PRIOR
            # contract forward untouched. Verdict/blockers above are recorded
            # unconditionally, so task-unchanged keeps working either way.
            baseline_res = next(
                (r for r in self.results if r.id == "baseline-clean"), None)
            baseline_ok = baseline_res is not None and baseline_res.status == PASS
            if baseline_ok:
                # Separate try per field (LOW8): a relative_to ValueError on an
                # absolute --target used to drop declared_scope AND
                # scaffold_sha256 while silently keeping baseline_commit.
                try:
                    st["baseline_commit"] = self.git("rev-parse", "HEAD")[1].strip()
                except Exception as e:
                    st["baseline_commit_error"] = f"{type(e).__name__}: {e}"
                try:
                    st["declared_scope"] = sorted(
                        self._rel_to_wt(f) for f in self.target_files())
                except Exception as e:
                    st["declared_scope_error"] = f"{type(e).__name__}: {e}"
                try:
                    st["scaffold_sha256"] = self.scaffold_digests()
                except Exception as e:
                    st["scaffold_sha256_error"] = f"{type(e).__name__}: {e}"
                st["untracked_before"] = sorted(
                    getattr(self, "untracked_before", set()))
            else:
                prior = self._prior_contract()
                for k in ("baseline_commit", "declared_scope",
                          "scaffold_sha256", "untracked_before"):
                    if prior.get(k) is not None:
                        st[k] = prior[k]
            # A review must NEVER rewrite the scope contract it is judging
            # against -- that would launder an out-of-scope change into the
            # declared scope and the next review would call it clean.
            if not review:
                sf.write_text(json.dumps(st, indent=2) + "\n")
                self.write_ledger(st)
        except Exception:
            pass

        if self.a.json:
            print(json.dumps({
                "verdict": verdict,
                "worktree": str(self.wt),
                "blockers": [r.as_dict() for r in blockers],
                "warnings": [r.as_dict() for r in warns],
                "unproven": [r.as_dict() for r in unproven],
                "checks": [r.as_dict() for r in self.results],
                "verify_seconds": self.verify_secs,
                "verify_relevance": (getattr(self, "_state", {}) or {}).get(
                    "verify_relevance"),
                # None means the baseline never ran -- distinct from False.
                # Consumers MUST treat None as "unknown", never as falsy.
                "verify_failed_at_baseline": (
                    None if self.baseline_rc is None
                    else self.baseline_rc != 0),
            }, indent=2))
            return 1 if blockers else 0

        C = {PASS: "\033[32m", FAIL: "\033[1;31m", WARN: "\033[33m",
             UNPROVEN: "\033[1;35m", SKIP: "\033[2m"} if sys.stdout.isatty() \
            else {PASS: "", FAIL: "", WARN: "", UNPROVEN: "", SKIP: ""}
        R = "\033[0m" if sys.stdout.isatty() else ""
        print(f"\n{'post-run review' if review else 'pre-flight'}: {self.wt}")
        print("-" * 72)
        for r in self.results:
            print(f"  {C[r.status]}[{r.status:4}]{R} {r.id:<18} {r.msg}")
            if r.detail and r.status in (FAIL, WARN, UNPROVEN):
                for line in _wrap(r.detail, 66):
                    print(f"         {C[SKIP]}{line}{R}")
            if r.fix and r.status in (FAIL, UNPROVEN):
                for line in _wrap("fix: " + r.fix, 66):
                    print(f"         {C[WARN]}{line}{R}")
        print("-" * 72)
        if blockers:
            head = blockers[0]
            print(f"{C[FAIL]}NO-GO{R}: {head.id} -- {head.msg}")
            if len(blockers) > 1:
                print(f"       plus {len(blockers)-1} more blocker(s): "
                      + ", ".join(r.id for r in blockers[1:]))
            if unproven:
                print(f"       ({len(unproven)} property also UNPROVEN: "
                      + ", ".join(r.id for r in unproven) + ")")
            print("       do NOT merge this diff as-is."
                  if review else "       nothing enqueued.")
        else:
            extra = []
            if unproven:
                extra.append(f"{len(unproven)} UNPROVEN")
            if warns:
                extra.append(f"{len(warns)} warning(s)")
            print(f"{C[PASS]}GO{R}" + (f" -- {', '.join(extra)}" if extra else ""))
            # Named individually, never as a count. A property nobody
            # established is not a property that held; collapsing the two is
            # how a gate starts certifying things it never looked at.
            for r in unproven:
                print(f"       {C[UNPROVEN]}UNPROVEN{R}: {r.id} -- {r.msg}")
            if unproven:
                print("       These were never established. If you enqueue "
                      "anyway, a later failure on any of them is YOURS, not "
                      "the model's.")
            if review:
                # A review is not an enqueue. Printing the enqueue command here
                # would invite re-dispatching the tree you just approved.
                # Only assert "scope held" when the audit actually RAN. If any
                # property is UNPROVEN (e.g. a never-gated tree with no recorded
                # contract), the scope audit did not establish anything and
                # claiming it did is a GO line asserting a property the run
                # never proved (MED5).
                if unproven:
                    print("       scope NOT established: "
                          + ", ".join(r.id for r in unproven)
                          + " UNPROVEN. The scope/scaffold audit did not run "
                            "(this tree may never have been gated), so nothing "
                            "here proves the change stayed in scope.")
                else:
                    print("       scope held: only the declared file(s) changed, "
                          "and the task/verify/fixtures are untouched.")
                print("       Still read the diff -- this proves scope, not "
                      "correctness.")
                return 0
            print("\n  python3 ~/bin/ollama-queue.py enqueue --model <tag> "
                  "--host <PIN> \\")
            print(f"    --cwd {self.wt} \\")
            print(f"    --task-file {self.wt / self.a.task_file} "
                  "--task-kind coding \\")
            print(f"    --verify {json.dumps(self.a.verify)} --num-ctx <fit> "
                  "--max-iters 30 --label <label>")
        return 1 if blockers else 0


def _wrap(text, width):
    words, line, out = text.split(), "", []
    for w in words:
        if len(line) + len(w) + 1 > width:
            out.append(line)
            line = w
        else:
            line = f"{line} {w}".strip()
    if line:
        out.append(line)
    return out


# ---- self-test ---------------------------------------------------------
# In-process end-to-end tests. Each builds a throwaway git repo, drives the
# gate, and asserts a specific guard bites. The two HIGH cases are written as
# revert-tests: the assertion is one that FAILS if the fix is undone.
_SELFTEST_DEFAULTS = dict(
    task_file="TASK.md", verify="bash verify.sh", refimpl=None,
    refimpl_cmd=None, no_refimpl=False, require=None, require_file=None,
    target=None, review=False, no_relevance=False, auto_seal=False,
    relevance_max_mutants=40, relevance_budget_s=600, json=False)

_ST_TASK = """# Task: tag the function

## Confirmed defect
Observed: target.py:2 returns 1 untagged. Reproduced by running `bash verify.sh`.

## Must contain
- `FIXED_MARKER`

## Loop
Run `bash verify.sh` after every edit until it prints VERIFY_OK.

## Scope
Only edit `target.py`; do not edit verify.sh.
"""

_ST_VERIFY = """#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -u
fails=0
if grep -q "FIXED_MARKER" target.py; then :; else echo "FAIL: marker absent"; fails=$((fails+1)); fi
echo "--- $fails failed ---"
if [ "$fails" -eq 0 ]; then echo VERIFY_OK; else exit 1; fi
"""


def _st_run_git(wt, *a):
    subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(wt), *a],
                   capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)


def _st_make_repo(wt):
    wt.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    (wt / "target.py").write_text("def f():\n    return 1\n")
    (wt / "verify.sh").write_text(_ST_VERIFY)
    (wt / "TASK.md").write_text(_ST_TASK)
    _st_run_git(wt, "init", "-q")
    _st_run_git(wt, "config", "user.email", "selftest@example.com")
    _st_run_git(wt, "config", "user.name", "selftest")
    _st_run_git(wt, "add", "-A")
    _st_run_git(wt, "commit", "-q", "-m", "baseline")


def _st_args(wt, **over):
    import argparse as _ap
    d = dict(_SELFTEST_DEFAULTS)
    d.update(over)
    return _ap.Namespace(worktree=str(wt), **d)


def _st_drive(wt, review=False, **over):
    """Run the gate (or review) in-process, capturing its stdout. Returns
    (rc, Preflight, printed_text)."""
    import contextlib, io
    pf = Preflight(_st_args(wt, review=review, **over))
    buf = io.StringIO()
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):
        rc = pf.run_review() if review else pf.run()
    return rc, pf, buf.getvalue()


def _st_state(wt):
    return json.loads((wt / STATE_FILE).read_text())


def _self_test():
    global PREFLIGHT_LEDGER, QUEUE_STATE
    import tempfile, shutil as _sh

    root = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="preflight-selftest-"))
    PREFLIGHT_LEDGER = root / "ledger"
    QUEUE_STATE = root / "queue.json"
    QUEUE_STATE.write_text(json.dumps({"jobs": []}))

    results = []

    def check(name, cond, extra=""):
        results.append((name, bool(cond), extra))

    def find(pf, cid):
        return next((r for r in pf.results if r.id == cid), None)

    try:
        # ---- HIGH1: contract NOT rewritten on a NO-GO baseline-clean run -----
        # Round 1: clean tree, gate records the contract. Round 2: tamper a
        # TRACKED scaffold file so baseline-clean FAILS, re-run (not --review),
        # and assert the recorded verify.sh digest did NOT move to the tampered
        # one. REVERT-TEST: with the HIGH1 fix removed, report() re-hashes the
        # current (tampered) files and this assertion goes red.
        wt = root / "high1"
        _st_make_repo(wt)
        _st_drive(wt, no_refimpl=True, no_relevance=True)
        st1 = _st_state(wt)
        orig = (st1.get("scaffold_sha256") or {}).get("verify.sh")
        check("HIGH1 round1 recorded a contract",
              bool(orig) and st1.get("declared_scope") == ["target.py"],
              f"scaffold={bool(orig)} scope={st1.get('declared_scope')}")
        # tamper a tracked scaffold file
        (wt / "verify.sh").write_text(_ST_VERIFY + "\n# tampered by the model\n")
        tampered_hash = hashlib.sha256((wt / "verify.sh").read_bytes()).hexdigest()
        rc2, pf2, _ = _st_drive(wt, no_refimpl=True, no_relevance=True)
        st2 = _st_state(wt)
        bclean = find(pf2, "baseline-clean")
        check("HIGH1 round2 baseline-clean FAILED",
              bclean is not None and bclean.status == FAIL)
        check("HIGH1 contract carried forward, NOT rewritten from tampered tree",
              (st2.get("scaffold_sha256") or {}).get("verify.sh") == orig
              and orig != tampered_hash,
              f"recorded={(st2.get('scaffold_sha256') or {}).get('verify.sh')} "
              f"orig={orig} tampered={tampered_hash}")
        check("HIGH1 verdict/blockers still recorded on the NO-GO",
              st2.get("verdict") == "NO-GO"
              and "baseline-clean" in (st2.get("blockers") or []))

        # ---- HIGH2: in-tree vs ledger mismatch -> FAIL contract-mismatch -----
        wt = root / "high2"
        _st_make_repo(wt)
        _st_drive(wt, no_refimpl=True, no_relevance=True)  # writes state + ledger
        # tamper ONLY the in-tree state: widen scope, re-hash the edited fixture
        stf = wt / STATE_FILE
        d = json.loads(stf.read_text())
        d["declared_scope"] = ["target.py", "evil.py"]
        sc = dict(d.get("scaffold_sha256") or {})
        sc["verify.sh"] = "deadbeef"
        d["scaffold_sha256"] = sc
        stf.write_text(json.dumps(d, indent=2))
        rc, pfr, _ = _st_drive(wt, review=True)
        cs = find(pfr, "contract-source")
        check("HIGH2 review FAILs contract-mismatch when in-tree disagrees "
              "with ledger",
              cs is not None and cs.status == FAIL
              and "contract-mismatch" in cs.msg)
        check("HIGH2 review verdict is NO-GO on the mismatch",
              any(r.status == FAIL for r in pfr.results))
        # REVERT-TEST evidence: if instead the in-tree file matched the ledger,
        # contract-source is PASS -- proving the FAIL is the mismatch, not noise.
        wt_ok = root / "high2ok"
        _st_make_repo(wt_ok)
        _st_drive(wt_ok, no_refimpl=True, no_relevance=True)
        rc, pfok, _ = _st_drive(wt_ok, review=True)
        csok = find(pfok, "contract-source")
        check("HIGH2 matching in-tree+ledger -> contract-source PASS (control)",
              csok is not None and csok.status == PASS)

        # ---- MED6: multi-file `Only edit` scope parsed fully -----------------
        wt = root / "med6"
        wt.mkdir()
        (wt / "a.py").write_text("x=1\n")
        (wt / "b.py").write_text("y=2\n")
        pf = Preflight(_st_args(wt))
        pf.task_text = "Intro. Only edit `a.py`, `b.py`; do not edit verify.sh.\n"
        names = sorted(p.name for p in pf.target_files())
        check("MED6 multi-backtick `a.py`, `b.py` -> both targets",
              names == ["a.py", "b.py"], f"got {names}")
        pf.task_text = "Only edit `a.py, b.py`.\n"  # comma inside one backtick
        names2 = sorted(p.name for p in pf.target_files())
        check("MED6 comma-in-one-backtick `a.py, b.py` -> both targets",
              names2 == ["a.py", "b.py"], f"got {names2}")

        # ---- MED5: "scope held" NOT printed while UNPROVEN -------------------
        wt = root / "med5"
        _st_make_repo(wt)  # never gated -> no state, no ledger entry
        rc, pf5, out5 = _st_drive(wt, review=True)
        check("MED5 never-gated review does NOT claim 'scope held'",
              "scope held" not in out5)
        check("MED5 never-gated review says scope NOT established / UNPROVEN",
              "scope NOT established" in out5 or "UNPROVEN" in out5)

        # ---- MED4 sanity: a root-level module the gate never saw -> FAIL -----
        wt = root / "med4"
        _st_make_repo(wt)
        _st_drive(wt, no_refimpl=True, no_relevance=True)
        (wt / "hashlib.py").write_text("# shadow\n")  # untracked, root-level
        rc, pf4, _ = _st_drive(wt, review=True)
        su = find(pf4, "scope-untracked")
        check("MED4 new root-level hashlib.py flagged FAIL",
              su is not None and su.status == FAIL, str(su and su.msg))

        # ---- LOW12: malformed queue entry does not crash cwd-exclusive ------
        QUEUE_STATE.write_text(json.dumps(
            {"jobs": ["not-a-dict", {"id": None, "status": "running",
                                     "cwd": str(root / "low12")}]}))
        wt = root / "low12"
        _st_make_repo(wt)
        try:
            rc, pf12, _ = _st_drive(wt, no_refimpl=True, no_relevance=True)
            ce = find(pf12, "cwd-exclusive")
            check("LOW12 cwd-exclusive survives a non-dict / null-id job",
                  ce is not None)
        except Exception as e:
            check("LOW12 cwd-exclusive survives a non-dict / null-id job",
                  False, f"raised {type(e).__name__}: {e}")
        QUEUE_STATE.write_text(json.dumps({"jobs": []}))

        # ---- LOW15: _norm_rel keeps a hidden file's dot ----------------------
        check("LOW15 _norm_rel('./.hidden.py') keeps the dot",
              _norm_rel("./.hidden.py") == ".hidden.py",
              _norm_rel("./.hidden.py"))
        check("LOW15 _norm_rel('./sub/x.py') strips only the ./",
              _norm_rel("./sub/x.py") == "sub/x.py")

        # ---- OVERCAPTURE: "do not edit `x`" tokens are NOT captured as targets
        # The REAL scaffold scope line backticks the fixture in a "do not edit"
        # clause; capturing those made the fixture a TARGET, which emptied
        # fixture_py_files()/fixture_js_files() and turned spec-gap AND
        # draft-confirmed inert on the standard shape. REVERT-TEST: undo the
        # target_files() truncation and verify.sh/test_fixture.py reappear here.
        wt = root / "overcapture"
        wt.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        for n in ("target.py", "verify.sh", "test_fixture.py"):
            (wt / n).write_text("x\n")
        pfoc = Preflight(_st_args(wt))
        pfoc.task_text = ("Only edit `target.py`; do not edit `verify.sh`, "
                          "`test_fixture.py` or `TASK.md`.\n")
        oc_names = sorted(p.name for p in pfoc.target_files())
        check("OVERCAPTURE 'do not edit' files excluded from targets",
              oc_names == ["target.py"], f"got {oc_names}")

        # ---- GAP1: draft-confirmed covers JS/TS fixtures, not just Python -----
        # An unconfirmed .mts/.mjs draft (const DRAFT_UNCONFIRMED = true) must
        # FAIL draft-confirmed exactly as a Python one does; a confirmed
        # (marker-stripped) fixture must not fire it. Pure text scan -> no
        # runtime needed, so these always run. REVERT-TEST: before the fix
        # check_draft_confirmed never looked at .mjs/.mts, so the "bad" cases
        # returned no result (r is None) and these assertions go red.
        def _mk_draft(dirname, fxname, marked):
            w = root / dirname
            w.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            (w / "verify.sh").write_text(
                "#!/usr/bin/env bash\nnode " + fxname + "\n")
            marker = "const DRAFT_UNCONFIRMED = true;\n" if marked else ""
            (w / fxname).write_text(marker + "const CASES = [];\n")
            # Scope names a target that is NOT the fixture, so target-inference
            # cannot confound what draft-confirmed sees.
            (w / "TASK.md").write_text("Task. Only edit `impl.ts`.\n")
            pf = Preflight(_st_args(w))
            pf.task_text = (w / "TASK.md").read_text()
            pf.check_draft_confirmed()
            return find(pf, "draft-confirmed")

        for suf in ("mts", "mjs"):
            fxname = f"verify_impl.{suf}"
            rbad = _mk_draft(f"gap1-{suf}-bad", fxname, marked=True)
            check(f"GAP1 unconfirmed .{suf} draft -> draft-confirmed FAILS",
                  rbad is not None and rbad.status == FAIL,
                  str(rbad and rbad.status))
            rok = _mk_draft(f"gap1-{suf}-ok", fxname, marked=False)
            check(f"GAP1 confirmed .{suf} draft -> draft-confirmed does NOT fire",
                  rok is None, str(rok and rok.status))

        # ---- GAP2: fixture_discriminates runs a JS fixture under node, not py -
        # Lives in ollama-dispatch-draft. Before the fix a .mjs ran under
        # sys.executable (python3) -> SyntaxError -> nonzero -> vacuous
        # "discriminates". After: node runs it and the real exit is read, so a
        # BENIGN .mjs (exits 0) is correctly caught as non-discriminating.
        # Guarded on node presence -> skip-with-notice, never a false pass.
        try:
            from importlib.machinery import SourceFileLoader as _SFL
            _draft = _SFL("odd_draft",
                          str(BIN / "ollama-dispatch-draft")).load_module()
        except Exception as e:
            _draft = None
            check("GAP2 ollama-dispatch-draft importable", False, str(e))
        if _draft is not None:
            if not shutil.which("node"):
                results.append(
                    ("GAP2 SKIPPED (node absent): fixture_discriminates JS "
                     "runtime unproven here", True, "node not on PATH"))
            else:
                wb = root / "gap2-benign"
                wb.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
                (wb / "verify_impl.mjs").write_text(
                    "const CASES = [];\nprocess.exit(0);\n")
                db, wdb = _draft.fixture_discriminates(wb)
                check("GAP2 benign .mjs (exits 0 under node) -> NOT "
                      "discriminating (was a vacuous True under python3)",
                      db is False, f"disc={db} why={str(wdb)[:60]}")
                wd = root / "gap2-disc"
                wd.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
                (wd / "verify_impl.mjs").write_text(
                    "const CASES = [];\nprocess.exit(1);\n")
                dd, wdd = _draft.fixture_discriminates(wd)
                check("GAP2 discriminating .mjs (exits 1 under node) -> "
                      "discriminates", dd is True, f"disc={dd} why={str(wdd)[:60]}")

        # ---- GAP3: .mts discrimination resolves tsx LOCALLY, and fails HONESTLY
        # when tsx is unavailable -- never the old silent `npx --yes tsx` that
        # needs the network and hangs/fails on the offline queue host.
        # Lives in ollama-dispatch-draft.resolve_tsx / fixture_discriminates.
        #  (a) tsx resolvable (vendored) -> a .mts that exits nonzero
        #      discriminates (True). Guarded: skip-with-notice if the vendored
        #      tsx isn't installed, so a bare checkout doesn't false-fail.
        #  (b) tsx forced-unavailable (vendored path bogus + which() stubbed so
        #      neither PATH-tsx nor an npx cache resolves) -> fixture_discriminates
        #      returns None (UNPROVEN), NOT a vacuous True and NOT a network
        #      attempt. This case ALWAYS runs. REVERT-TEST: restore the old
        #      `else ["npx","--yes","tsx"]` and (b) would try to spawn npx
        #      (network) instead of returning the honest None.
        if _draft is not None:
            tcmd, tsrc = _draft.resolve_tsx(root / "no-such-wt")
            if tcmd is not None and not str(tsrc).startswith("npx"):
                wm = root / "gap3-mts-disc"
                wm.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
                # CASES_AUTHORED guard is how fixture_of() finds a .mts; the
                # typed `: number` proves tsx type-strips (bare node/py can't).
                (wm / "verify_impl.mts").write_text(
                    "const CASES_AUTHORED: boolean = true;\nprocess.exit(1);\n")
                dm, wdm = _draft.fixture_discriminates(wm)
                check("GAP3 .mts (exits 1 under vendored tsx) -> discriminates "
                      "locally, no network", dm is True,
                      f"disc={dm} why={str(wdm)[:80]} src={tsrc}")
            else:
                results.append(
                    ("GAP3 SKIPPED (local tsx absent): .mts local-discrimination "
                     "unproven here -- run `cd ~/bin/ts-mutator && npm install`",
                     True, f"resolve_tsx={tsrc}"))
            # (b) honest failure when tsx is genuinely unavailable -- ALWAYS runs.
            _saved_vendor = _draft.TSX_VENDOR
            _saved_which = _draft.shutil.which
            try:
                _draft.TSX_VENDOR = root / "definitely" / "no" / "tsx"
                _draft.shutil.which = lambda _n: None  # no PATH tsx, no npx
                wu = root / "gap3-mts-unavail"
                wu.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
                (wu / "verify_impl.mts").write_text(
                    "const CASES_AUTHORED: boolean = true;\nprocess.exit(1);\n")
                du, wdu = _draft.fixture_discriminates(wu)
                check("GAP3 tsx forced-unavailable -> fixture_discriminates is "
                      "UNPROVEN (None), not a vacuous pass or network attempt",
                      du is None and "tsx not resolvable" in str(wdu),
                      f"disc={du} why={str(wdu)[:80]}")
            finally:
                _draft.TSX_VENDOR = _saved_vendor
                _draft.shutil.which = _saved_which
    finally:
        try:
            _sh.rmtree(root)
        except Exception:
            pass

    passed = sum(1 for _, ok, _ in results if ok)
    total = len(results)
    for name, ok, extra in results:
        tag = "PASS" if ok else "FAIL"
        line = f"  [{tag}] {name}"
        if not ok and extra:
            line += f"   ({extra})"
        print(line)
    print("-" * 60)
    print(f"self-test: {passed}/{total} passed")
    return 0 if passed == total else 1


def main():
    # Intercept --self-test before argparse, which otherwise requires the
    # `worktree` positional. Runs the in-process guard tests and exits.
    if "--self-test" in sys.argv[1:]:
        return _self_test()
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="GO/NO-GO gate for an ollama dispatch worktree.",
        epilog="exit 0 = GO, 1 = NO-GO, 2 = usage error")
    ap.add_argument("worktree", help="worktree containing TASK.md + verify.sh")
    ap.add_argument("--self-test", action="store_true",
                    help="run the in-process guard self-tests and exit")
    ap.add_argument("--task-file", default="TASK.md")
    ap.add_argument("--verify", default="bash verify.sh")
    g = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    g.add_argument("--refimpl", help="reference-impl patch (git apply / patch -p1)")
    g.add_argument("--refimpl-cmd", help="shell command that writes the reference "
                                         "impl into the worktree")
    g.add_argument("--no-refimpl", action="store_true",
                   help="EXPLICIT WAIVER of the both-ways proof. Recorded in the "
                        "verdict as UNPROVEN; the task may be unsatisfiable and "
                        "you will not find out until the model has burned its "
                        "iterations on it.")
    ap.add_argument("--require", action="append", metavar="LITERAL",
                    help="a literal your last edit added to the task; asserted "
                         "present. Repeatable. This is the edit-landed guard.")
    ap.add_argument("--require-file", help="file of --require literals, one/line")
    ap.add_argument("--target", action="append",
                    help="file the model is told to edit (inferred from the task "
                         "otherwise)")
    ap.add_argument("--review", action="store_true",
                    help="POST-RUN audit of a finished dispatch instead of the "
                         "pre-flight. READ-ONLY. Holds the tree against the "
                         "scope contract the gate recorded before dispatch: "
                         "did anything TRACKED change outside the declared "
                         "target, and are the task/verify/fixtures still "
                         "byte-identical. A green verify covers neither.")
    ap.add_argument("--no-relevance", action="store_true",
                    help="EXPLICIT WAIVER of the mutation-relevance check. "
                         "Recorded as UNPROVEN; the dispatch can never "
                         "auto-approve.")
    ap.add_argument("--auto-seal", action="store_true",
                    help="if the authored harness is not sealed into the launch "
                         "baseline (untracked/modified in a linked worktree), "
                         "commit it here instead of NO-GOing. Default is a loud "
                         "NO-GO with the exact `--seal-baseline` command.")
    ap.add_argument("--relevance-max-mutants", type=int, default=40)
    ap.add_argument("--relevance-budget-s", type=int, default=600)
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    # --review audits a FINISHED run; there is no both-ways proof to do and
    # nothing to apply -- demanding a refimpl here would only tempt an operator
    # into pointing a tree-mutating flag at a tree they are trying to inspect.
    if not args.review and not (args.refimpl or args.refimpl_cmd
                                or args.no_refimpl):
        ap.error("the both-ways proof needs a reference impl: pass --refimpl "
                 "<patch>, --refimpl-cmd '<cmd>', or waive it explicitly with "
                 "--no-refimpl")
    if args.review and (args.refimpl or args.refimpl_cmd):
        ap.error("--review is READ-ONLY and never applies a reference impl; "
                 "drop --refimpl/--refimpl-cmd")
    try:
        pf = Preflight(args)
        return pf.run_review() if args.review else pf.run()
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        print("\ninterrupted -- the worktree may still have the reference impl "
              "applied; check `git status` before enqueuing.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
